WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.952 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.952 --> 00:00:03.416 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.416 --> 00:00:04.880 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:12.690 align:middle line:90%


00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.160 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: Let me first
start with some administration.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:20.585 align:middle line:84%
So actually, some of you noticed
that the pset problem number two

00:00:20.585 --> 00:00:23.610 align:middle line:90%
B, there is a slight problem.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:27.110 align:middle line:84%
I posted a comment about
this in the website.

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:30.060 align:middle line:90%


00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.950 align:middle line:84%
So there's an
assumption I want you

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.570 align:middle line:84%
to make for problem
two B. Just look

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:34.780 align:middle line:84%
at the website and
the announcement.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:36.363 align:middle line:84%
If you have any
questions, come to me.

00:00:36.363 --> 00:00:37.630 align:middle line:90%
So that's one thing.

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:40.710 align:middle line:90%
The other thing is in--

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.410 align:middle line:84%
well, next week is Thanksgiving,
so happy Thanksgiving.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:49.150 align:middle line:84%
And the week after, I'm actually
away giving some keynote talk.

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:51.360 align:middle line:84%
But actually, it
worked out really

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.040 align:middle line:84%
well because the
topic of that lecture

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.110 align:middle line:84%
is going to be
about what's called

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.630 align:middle line:90%
BARGs, or batch arguments.

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.880 align:middle line:84%
That would lead us kind
of in the final class

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.450 align:middle line:84%
to get our SNARGs that we
were kind of building for.

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:17.645 align:middle line:84%
And that lecture covers a work
by three authors, Jain Jin,

00:01:17.645 --> 00:01:19.910 align:middle line:90%
Choudhuri, Jain Jin.

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:23.427 align:middle line:84%
And Jain Jin is going to come
here and present the results.

00:01:23.427 --> 00:01:26.010 align:middle line:84%
So you'll get the author kind
of presenting the relevant work.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.340 align:middle line:84%
And then the final class,
we'll kind wrap everything up

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:33.110 align:middle line:84%
and show you how to get what we
worked for this entire semester.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:36.620 align:middle line:84%
So just of what we're
going to focus on

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.030 align:middle line:84%
for today-- so any questions
about administration,

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.030 align:middle line:84%
about questions from
last class, anything

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.165 align:middle line:90%
before we go into the material?

00:01:43.165 --> 00:01:45.910 align:middle line:90%


00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:46.540 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.780 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, when I'm
doing the homework, I realized

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:50.980 align:middle line:90%
there's one thing.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.250 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure if that's something
you changed with gk alpha.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:02:01.022 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] Is there any way
to get subtraction to work?

00:02:01.022 --> 00:02:02.230 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: So let's talk.

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:03.730 align:middle line:90%
Come to me after class.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:04.600 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:05.800 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:06.790 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:08.570 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:09.990 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk about
this after class.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:13.190 align:middle line:90%
So what's the plan for today?

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:15.980 align:middle line:84%
Or where are we kind
of left off last class

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:20.240 align:middle line:84%
and where we're heading
for today, so last class

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.440 align:middle line:84%
we defined the
Fiat-Shamir paradigm.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.430 align:middle line:84%
So kind of from the
beginning, what did we do?

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:27.380 align:middle line:84%
We showed this
interactive protocols.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.850 align:middle line:84%
And then we showed,
for example, GKR,

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.730 align:middle line:84%
which is only for bounded
depth computations.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:37.010 align:middle line:84%
Then we showed you can build
from that a PCP and then use

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:40.400 align:middle line:84%
that PCP to shrink
it using cryptography

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.540 align:middle line:90%
and get actually four message--

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.530 align:middle line:84%
That was the key in
Micali protocol--

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:48.470 align:middle line:84%
protocol, proofs, or arguments,
computationally sound

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:53.040 align:middle line:84%
proofs, for all of NP
where it's very succinct.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.330 align:middle line:84%
So we can take any
witness for NP.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:58.800 align:middle line:84%
And instead of sending this
witness, which is long,

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.530 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, poly
n bits, I'm going

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.530 align:middle line:84%
to shrink it to be an
interactive protocol, so

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.660 align:middle line:90%
four messages, to get it--

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.940 align:middle line:84%
the number of bits is
proportional to the security

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.958 align:middle line:84%
parameter, which can
be much, much smaller.

00:03:10.958 --> 00:03:12.000 align:middle line:90%
So that's what we showed.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.500 align:middle line:84%
That was the
Kilian-Micali protocol,

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:14.640 align:middle line:90%
but that was for messages.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.220 align:middle line:84%
So now, we're kind of
towards the homestretch.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:19.250 align:middle line:84%
We want to get around
these four messages

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:22.490 align:middle line:84%
and reduce it down
to just one message.

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:23.850 align:middle line:90%
So how do we do that?

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:27.140 align:middle line:84%
So last class, we defined
this paradigm called

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:28.500 align:middle line:90%
the Fiat-Shamir paradigm.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.260 align:middle line:84%
It was introduced
exactly for this purpose

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:36.650 align:middle line:84%
to eliminate interaction
from interactive protocols

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:41.130 align:middle line:84%
by essentially replacing the
verifier with a hash function.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:43.290 align:middle line:90%
And now, there was a question.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.410 align:middle line:84%
It's a very nice, very simple,
very efficient kind of paradigm,

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:47.612 align:middle line:90%
very elegant, in my opinion.

00:03:47.612 --> 00:03:49.320 align:middle line:84%
But then there's a
question, is it sound?

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.350 align:middle line:84%
If you start with an
interactive protocol

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:52.850 align:middle line:84%
that you can prove
soundness, when

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:54.360 align:middle line:84%
you apply the
Fiat-Shamir paradigm,

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.490 align:middle line:84%
is the protocol or
the proof still sound?

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:57.540 align:middle line:90%
That's the question.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:01.230 align:middle line:84%
And what we proved
for this protocol,

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:03.410 align:middle line:84%
Kilian-Micali protocol,
is it is sound

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:05.130 align:middle line:84%
if you apply the
Fiat-Shamir paradigm.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:10.560 align:middle line:84%
But this hash function
is a random oracle.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.580 align:middle line:84%
So instead of being actually
an explicit kind of circuit,

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.209 align:middle line:84%
if we think of it as a
completely random function,

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:20.519 align:middle line:84%
that both parties have oracle
access to, then it's sound.

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:22.718 align:middle line:84%
But in real world,
there is no kind

00:04:22.718 --> 00:04:25.260 align:middle line:84%
of random oracles in the sky
that all parties have access to.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:30.360 align:middle line:84%
We have to use specific circuits
to replace this hash function.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.660 align:middle line:84%
And then there was
this question of,

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:35.270 align:middle line:84%
when we actually use
the Fiat-Shamir paradigm

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:40.460 align:middle line:84%
with a specific circuit,
with a specific hash, that's

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:45.020 align:middle line:84%
computable by a specific
kind of circuit, is it sound?

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.790 align:middle line:84%
And that was kind of
open for a long time.

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:50.540 align:middle line:84%
It was a very, very
important open problem

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.070 align:middle line:84%
because Fiat-Shamir was used
all over the place in practice.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:56.700 align:middle line:84%
So understanding whether it's
secure or not is very important.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:01.910 align:middle line:84%
And we could only analyze it in
the random oracle model, so not

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:04.080 align:middle line:90%
in real world settings.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:07.370 align:middle line:84%
And as I pointed out last
time, the random oracle model

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:09.480 align:middle line:84%
actually worked out to
be a very good proxy.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.407 align:middle line:84%
So none of the schemes
in practice actually

00:05:11.407 --> 00:05:13.490 align:middle line:84%
that we proved security
in the random oracle model

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:15.030 align:middle line:90%
turned out to be broken.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:19.250 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out it's a very,
very good proxy for security.

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:22.380 align:middle line:84%
But still, at least for
me as a theoretician,

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:26.270 align:middle line:84%
I want to understand,
when we replace it

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.890 align:middle line:84%
with an actual function,
an actual program,

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:29.940 align:middle line:90%
why is it secure.

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:33.020 align:middle line:84%
And frankly, I still
don't understand

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:34.290 align:middle line:90%
why it's such a good proxy.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:35.180 align:middle line:90%
I have to say.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:38.690 align:middle line:84%
It's surprising to me
that never, ever we had--

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:46.920 align:middle line:84%
it's really, really
approximates security very well.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.260 align:middle line:84%
Even though, as I mentioned,
we do have counterexamples.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:53.010 align:middle line:84%
We do have examples contrived,
not real world examples,

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:56.030 align:middle line:84%
but contrived examples
where the protocol are

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:58.970 align:middle line:84%
secure in the random
oracle model and not secure

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:02.340 align:middle line:84%
when applied Fiat-Shamir
And actually, interestingly,

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:04.160 align:middle line:90%
Kilian-Micali is one of them.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:05.810 align:middle line:84%
It's actually one
of the protocols

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:09.420 align:middle line:84%
where actually it
was proven that,

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:12.750 align:middle line:84%
if you use a certain
hash function--

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.550 align:middle line:84%
collision resistant,
but certain hash

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:17.520 align:middle line:84%
function-- then actually
you can't prove security.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:19.080 align:middle line:84%
No matter which
specific function

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:25.290 align:middle line:84%
you use for the Fiat-Shamir
function, it will not be secure.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:28.210 align:middle line:84%
But this specific hash function,
which is collision resistant,

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:29.140 align:middle line:90%
is contrived.

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:32.790 align:middle line:84%
So we still don't have real
world natural application

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:35.190 align:middle line:84%
of where security was proven
in the random oracle model

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:38.000 align:middle line:84%
but turned out to be
false in the real world.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.474 align:middle line:90%


00:06:42.474 --> 00:06:44.180 align:middle line:84%
So that's where
we're heading to,

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:46.790 align:middle line:84%
trying to prove the
security of or the soundness

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:49.190 align:middle line:90%
of the Fiat-Shamir paradigm.

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:52.340 align:middle line:84%
And along the way,
what we did is

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:56.070 align:middle line:84%
I defined a certain protocol,
which is zero knowledge.

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:58.740 align:middle line:84%
We kind of took a detour,
talked about zero knowledge.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.100 align:middle line:84%
And we looked at the specific
protocols, zero knowledge

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:06.440 align:middle line:84%
protocol, for Hamiltonicity,
for whether a graph

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:08.190 align:middle line:90%
has a Hamiltonian cycle or not.

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:10.040 align:middle line:90%
That's an NP complete problem.

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:13.490 align:middle line:84%
And so here is just a recap
because we'll look at it again

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:14.140 align:middle line:90%
today.

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:18.870 align:middle line:84%
So here is a proof, a
three-message proof system.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:21.170 align:middle line:84%
The prover is trying to
prove to the verifier

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:24.470 align:middle line:84%
that a graph G has
a Hamiltonian cycle.

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:26.430 align:middle line:84%
And we want to do it
in zero knowledge,

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:28.440 align:middle line:84%
so in a proof that
gives no information.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:32.150 align:middle line:84%
And the way we did it, we said,
OK, the prover, so the prover

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:33.310 align:middle line:90%
has a Hamiltonian cycle.

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:35.060 align:middle line:84%
He does not want to
reveal any information

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:38.060 align:middle line:84%
about this Hamiltonian
cycle to the verifier.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:39.730 align:middle line:90%
What would the prover do?

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:43.430 align:middle line:84%
He will choose a
random permutation, pi.

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:48.140 align:middle line:84%
He will commit, put in
a safe, locked safe,

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:52.810 align:middle line:84%
only the Hamiltonian
cycle of pi of g.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:56.840 align:middle line:84%
So if c is the path
Hamiltonian cycle in g,

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:03.590 align:middle line:84%
he will compute pi of the cycle,
so the cycle inside pi of g.

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:05.780 align:middle line:84%
And then he's going
to get a random bit.

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:07.550 align:middle line:84%
And he's going to
give an answer.

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:09.110 align:middle line:90%
What is the answer?

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:14.230 align:middle line:84%
If the bit is zero, he's
just going to open the cycle.

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:16.210 align:middle line:90%
Hi.

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:19.420 align:middle line:84%
He's just going
to open the cycle.

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:23.140 align:middle line:90%
Or maybe I should say pi of c.

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:27.182 align:middle line:84%
So he's going to open only the
places where there's a cycle.

00:08:27.182 --> 00:08:28.640 align:middle line:84%
And the guy is
going to check that,

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:31.532 align:middle line:90%
yes, it's an, indeed, cycle.

00:08:31.532 --> 00:08:33.740 align:middle line:84%
But we need to make sure
that the cycle has something

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:35.210 align:middle line:90%
to do with the graph itself.

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:40.280 align:middle line:84%
So if the b is 1,
then we check, indeed,

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:43.429 align:middle line:90%
that you give the permutation.

00:08:43.429 --> 00:08:45.060 align:middle line:84%
You do not give the
permitted cycle.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:46.560 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, it will
give information.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:48.620 align:middle line:84%
So you only give
them the permutation.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:53.870 align:middle line:84%
And you open only
nonedges in pi of g.

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:59.030 align:middle line:84%
So now you know everywhere in
pi of g that there is no edge.

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:01.930 align:middle line:84%
In this commitment,
there is no edges.

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:04.600 align:middle line:84%
So again, let me just
emphasize to recap.

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:10.940 align:middle line:84%
This commitment, if n is the
number of nodes in the graph,

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:12.970 align:middle line:84%
it's committing to
n squared values

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:16.270 align:middle line:84%
where there's a 1 if there's an
edge, 0 if there's not an edge.

00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:19.640 align:middle line:84%
For any ij, it says 01 whether
it's an edge or not an edge.

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:24.460 align:middle line:84%
And if b equals 0, we
only open the places

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:26.330 align:middle line:84%
where there's a
Hamiltonian cycle.

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:28.298 align:middle line:84%
We check that everything
there should be 1.

00:09:28.298 --> 00:09:29.840 align:middle line:84%
And you check that
it kind of closes,

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:31.790 align:middle line:90%
that it corresponds to a cycle.

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:35.030 align:middle line:84%
And if b equals 1, you give
the entire permutation,

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:38.140 align:middle line:90%
and you open the nonedges.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:40.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, the claim was that this
is, indeed, zero knowledge.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:43.782 align:middle line:84%
And the intuition was,
well, if you open zero,

00:09:43.782 --> 00:09:45.490 align:middle line:84%
he doesn't learn
anything because he just

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:46.323 align:middle line:90%
gets a random cycle.

00:09:46.323 --> 00:09:47.860 align:middle line:84%
It has nothing to
do with the graph.

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:49.990 align:middle line:84%
Just any random cycle,
that's what he sees.

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:53.690 align:middle line:84%
He could have information
about the graph.

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:56.250 align:middle line:84%
And if he gets b equals
1, then what does he see?

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:58.580 align:middle line:84%
A random permutation and a
bunch of openings of zero,

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:01.680 align:middle line:90%
that's no information.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:04.378 align:middle line:84%
And as we said, the
soundness here is only half.

00:10:04.378 --> 00:10:05.170 align:middle line:90%
That's the problem.

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:06.503 align:middle line:90%
So with the half, you can cheat.

00:10:06.503 --> 00:10:07.680 align:middle line:90%
Because if you guessed--

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:10.320 align:middle line:84%
a cheating prover,
I can guess b.

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:13.590 align:middle line:84%
And if I guess b, I can
at least cheat on that b.

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:16.800 align:middle line:90%


00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:19.720 align:middle line:84%
Even if g doesn't have
a Hamiltonian cycle,

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:24.290 align:middle line:84%
I can always answer correctly
on one of them, but not both.

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:26.190 align:middle line:84%
So if I guess your
b, I can answer.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:27.030 align:middle line:90%
I can cheat you.

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:30.950 align:middle line:84%
So this gives only
soundness half.

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:32.870 align:middle line:90%
This is just a recap.

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:35.060 align:middle line:84%
I motivated this to
show that this is not

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:36.750 align:middle line:84%
even secure in the
random oracle model.

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:39.890 align:middle line:84%
So actually, to get security
even in the random oracle model,

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:42.670 align:middle line:90%
you need to repeat in parallel.

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:47.290 align:middle line:84%
So today, when I say
now P, V, like this,

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:50.860 align:middle line:84%
so when I say like
the protocol P, V,

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:53.440 align:middle line:84%
I want you to-- throughout
this class, let's think of it

00:10:53.440 --> 00:11:02.490 align:middle line:84%
as parallel repetition of this
with security parameter lambda

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:03.600 align:middle line:90%
copies.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:07.010 align:middle line:84%
So now, I mean, some of the
stuff is really not good for us.

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:08.010 align:middle line:90%
That's not what we want.

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:11.790 align:middle line:84%
So think of it that we repeat
this in parallel security

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:14.130 align:middle line:90%
parameter number of times.

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:17.730 align:middle line:84%
So in other words, the
prover lambda number of times

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:22.270 align:middle line:84%
each time chooses a fresh
permutation pi, commits.

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:26.130 align:middle line:84%
So he does this lambda times
each time with a new permutation

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:26.630 align:middle line:90%
pi.

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:27.460 align:middle line:90%
This is very important.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:28.900 align:middle line:84%
And people see
why it's important

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:32.020 align:middle line:84%
that the permutation
is fresh each time?

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:34.940 align:middle line:84%
If you use the same permutation
and you open b0, b1,

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:36.540 align:middle line:90%
you learn everything.

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:39.910 align:middle line:84%
So each time you give a fresh
kind of random permutation,

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:43.510 align:middle line:84%
you send it lambda times,
you get a b for each one.

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:45.710 align:middle line:84%
And now, you send a
message for each one.

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:48.370 align:middle line:90%


00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:50.460 align:middle line:90%
So that was the protocol.

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:54.050 align:middle line:84%
Now, we proved that it's secure
in the random oracle model.

00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:59.310 align:middle line:84%
Actually, we proved that
any comes around protocol

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:02.670 align:middle line:84%
that has negligible soundness
is secure in the random oracle

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:03.250 align:middle line:90%
model.

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:04.330 align:middle line:90%
We showed that last time.

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:07.220 align:middle line:84%
So it's secure in the
random oracle model.

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:10.820 align:middle line:84%
And now, there is a question, is
it secure in the standard model

00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:14.540 align:middle line:90%
when we use Fiat-Shamir?

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:18.283 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going to show
today is, yes, it is secure.

00:12:18.283 --> 00:12:19.700 align:middle line:84%
So that's kind of
the first thing.

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:22.130 align:middle line:84%
We're going to show that
this protocol, P and V--

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:24.660 align:middle line:84%
and, again, when I say P and
V throughout today's class,

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:27.032 align:middle line:84%
it's the parallel
repeated version.

00:12:27.032 --> 00:12:30.680 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to show that P
and V are secure when you apply

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:35.057 align:middle line:84%
Fiat-Shamir in the standard
model for some commitment, not

00:12:35.057 --> 00:12:35.765 align:middle line:90%
every commitment.

00:12:35.765 --> 00:12:37.370 align:middle line:84%
So we'll open this
box a little bit

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:41.610 align:middle line:84%
and show that this is a very
natural commitment scheme.

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:43.250 align:middle line:84%
But with that
commitment scheme, we

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:45.560 align:middle line:84%
can argue that
this is secure when

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:47.090 align:middle line:84%
the hash function--
we'll actually

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:48.440 align:middle line:90%
construct a hash function.

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:49.940 align:middle line:84%
I'll give you an
exact hash function

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:52.770 align:middle line:84%
and say, if you use this hash
function for the Fiat-Shamir,

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:55.730 align:middle line:84%
you will get security
or soundness.

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:58.205 align:middle line:84%
But this hash
function, to argue,

00:12:58.205 --> 00:13:00.080 align:middle line:84%
you need to assume
something about the hash--

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:03.890 align:middle line:84%
I mean, this hash function
assumes some cryptography.

00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:06.320 align:middle line:84%
And the cryptography
it assumes is

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:08.700 align:middle line:84%
the existence of a fully
homomorphic encryption.

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:09.830 align:middle line:90%
I'll define what that is.

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:12.080 align:middle line:84%
But not just a fully
homomorphic encryption,

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:15.600 align:middle line:84%
we'll assume, also, that
it's circular secure fully

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:16.900 align:middle line:90%
homomorphic encryption.

00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:18.450 align:middle line:84%
I'll define what
that is as well.

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:20.045 align:middle line:84%
I just want to say
actually, today, we

00:13:20.045 --> 00:13:21.670 align:middle line:84%
don't need the circular
secure version.

00:13:21.670 --> 00:13:22.878 align:middle line:90%
We know how to get rid of it.

00:13:22.878 --> 00:13:25.955 align:middle line:84%
But what I'm going to explain
today does use this version.

00:13:25.955 --> 00:13:26.705 align:middle line:90%
It's just simpler.

00:13:26.705 --> 00:13:30.420 align:middle line:90%


00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:31.450 align:middle line:90%
We believe this is true.

00:13:31.450 --> 00:13:34.200 align:middle line:84%
There's an instantiation based
on circular secure Learning

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:35.310 align:middle line:90%
With Error, LWE.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:38.280 align:middle line:84%
But we don't need to
get into these details.

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:41.880 align:middle line:84%
So that's what we're going to do
in the first part of the class.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:43.600 align:middle line:84%
And the second
part of the class--

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:46.050 align:middle line:84%
so now, you can say, OK, let's
say we prove Fiat-Shamir.

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:47.640 align:middle line:84%
Why am I obsessed
with this protocol?

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:49.848 align:middle line:84%
And let's say we prove
Fiat-Shamir for this protocol.

00:13:49.848 --> 00:13:51.010 align:middle line:90%
So what?

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:52.100 align:middle line:90%
Why should you be engaged?

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:54.600 align:middle line:84%
So let me try to give you a
motivation for you to stay awake

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:55.420 align:middle line:90%
and listen to me.

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:56.710 align:middle line:90%
So why is it?

00:13:56.710 --> 00:14:00.700 align:middle line:84%
So this protocol, in
particular, is very important.

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:03.000 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:05.670 align:middle line:84%
What I'm going to show is
when you apply Fiat-Shamir

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:09.090 align:middle line:84%
to this protocol, you get what's
called a Noninteractive Zero

00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:12.200 align:middle line:90%
Knowledge proof, NIZK for short.

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:17.245 align:middle line:84%
So once we do that for this
protocol, because it was zero

00:14:17.245 --> 00:14:19.620 align:middle line:84%
knowledge, we can argue that,
when you apply Fiat-Shamir,

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:21.300 align:middle line:84%
you get noninteractive
zero knowledge.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:24.450 align:middle line:84%
And to get noninteractive zero
knowledge based on lattices

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:27.960 align:middle line:84%
was an open question for
a very, very long time.

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:29.455 align:middle line:84%
And this was resolved
in this paper

00:14:29.455 --> 00:14:31.080 align:middle line:84%
that I'm going to
present, in this work

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:36.640 align:middle line:84%
that I'm going to present to you
by Canetti, Lombardi and Wichs.

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:39.660 align:middle line:84%
So one of the
motivating examples

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:42.280 align:middle line:84%
is that we get this
noninteractive zero knowledge.

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:43.800 align:middle line:90%
That's one.

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:47.880 align:middle line:84%
But the other motivating example
is that actually this technique

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:49.190 align:middle line:90%
is much more general.

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:52.140 align:middle line:84%
The technique of Fiat-Shamir
that I'm going to show you

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:54.530 align:middle line:84%
is much more general than
just for this protocol.

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:56.640 align:middle line:84%
Actually, it's a very
general technique that

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:58.450 align:middle line:90%
can be used for many protocols.

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:01.690 align:middle line:84%
And in particular, we used it
later, this exact technique.

00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:03.390 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to
try to show you how

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:08.520 align:middle line:84%
it can be used to show that the
G-CARE protocol that we studied

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:10.560 align:middle line:84%
in the beginning
actually is secure.

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:13.440 align:middle line:90%


00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:15.160 align:middle line:84%
When you apply
Fiat-Shamir to it,

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:18.458 align:middle line:84%
you get security under
learning with error, or FHE.

00:15:18.458 --> 00:15:20.250 align:middle line:84%
Again, as I mentioned,
this proven security

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:21.300 align:middle line:90%
can be prevented.

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:24.760 align:middle line:84%
I'm just showing you the result
that uses it for simplicity.

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:28.460 align:middle line:84%
So it's not just for this
protocol to get NIZK.

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:30.130 align:middle line:84%
You can actually use
it to get SNARGs.

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:34.380 align:middle line:84%
And moreover, what you'll
see after Thanksgiving

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:36.070 align:middle line:84%
that Jain Jin is
going to present,

00:15:36.070 --> 00:15:37.950 align:middle line:84%
he's going to use
this exact same tool

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:40.870 align:middle line:90%
to get batch arguments for NP.

00:15:40.870 --> 00:15:46.270 align:middle line:84%
So this kind of Fiat-Shamir
is baked into all our results

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:47.320 align:middle line:90%
almost.

00:15:47.320 --> 00:15:50.830 align:middle line:84%
So it's actually very
general and nice.

00:15:50.830 --> 00:15:52.166 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:15:52.166 --> 00:15:56.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How does
that relate to--

00:15:56.410 --> 00:16:00.280 align:middle line:84%
because last time, we went
a little bit over the result

00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:03.160 align:middle line:90%
of [INAUDIBLE]--

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:04.460 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Great question.

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:05.840 align:middle line:90%
Great, great, great question.

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:06.340 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:07.990 align:middle line:84%
Let me repeat your
question because you

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:09.050 align:middle line:90%
have a great question.

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:11.067 align:middle line:84%
So last time, I
kind of showed you

00:16:11.067 --> 00:16:12.400 align:middle line:90%
that there's actually a tension.

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:15.960 align:middle line:84%
If you have an interactive
proof that is zero knowledge,

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:20.700 align:middle line:84%
then I kind of gave you this
high-level intuition that,

00:16:20.700 --> 00:16:24.570 align:middle line:84%
if it is really zero knowledge,
you cannot apply Fiat-Shamir

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:25.130 align:middle line:90%
to it.

00:16:25.130 --> 00:16:27.010 align:middle line:90%
It will fail.

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:30.280 align:middle line:84%
And here, I'm saying,
oh, we're going

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:31.580 align:middle line:90%
to apply Fiat-Shamir to it.

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:33.650 align:middle line:84%
And not only that,
we're going to get

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:34.990 align:middle line:90%
noninteractive zero knowledge.

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:36.657 align:middle line:84%
It's like, wait, wait,
wait, wait, wait.

00:16:36.657 --> 00:16:39.410 align:middle line:84%
Last time, you said you
can't get your knowledge.

00:16:39.410 --> 00:16:42.930 align:middle line:84%
And I'm kind of
contradicting myself here.

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:46.410 align:middle line:84%
So again, let me repeat it just
to make sure we're all on board.

00:16:46.410 --> 00:16:50.450 align:middle line:84%
Last time, I said a protocol
that is zero knowledge cannot be

00:16:50.450 --> 00:16:52.970 align:middle line:90%
Fiat-Shamir secure.

00:16:52.970 --> 00:16:54.870 align:middle line:90%
Now, I'm saying, guess what?

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:57.860 align:middle line:84%
This protocol is
Fiat-Shamir secure.

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:02.710 align:middle line:84%
So first of all,
indeed, I have no reason

00:17:02.710 --> 00:17:06.710 align:middle line:84%
to believe that this protocol,
when you repeat it in parallel,

00:17:06.710 --> 00:17:07.880 align:middle line:90%
that it's zero knowledge.

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:15.150 align:middle line:84%
We know that it's zero knowledge
if without parallel repetition.

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:18.630 align:middle line:84%
We know that it's zero knowledge
if you repeat it sequentially.

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:20.579 align:middle line:84%
If you repeat it in
parallel, we don't

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:22.780 align:middle line:84%
know that it's zero
knowledge anymore.

00:17:22.780 --> 00:17:23.890 align:middle line:90%
That's first.

00:17:23.890 --> 00:17:27.760 align:middle line:84%
Second, next thing you
can say is, oh, so now we

00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:30.280 align:middle line:84%
know that it's not
zero knowledge.

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:32.210 align:middle line:90%
Almost, almost.

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:35.230 align:middle line:84%
Actually, the result does show
that parallel repetition does

00:17:35.230 --> 00:17:36.880 align:middle line:90%
not preserve zero knowledge.

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:38.590 align:middle line:84%
But actually, it's
not going to be

00:17:38.590 --> 00:17:42.400 align:middle line:84%
completely because I'm going to
change the commitment in a way

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:45.440 align:middle line:84%
that you'll see in a
minute, so not quite.

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:51.010 align:middle line:84%
But then you can say, OK, but
how do you get NIZK out of it?

00:17:51.010 --> 00:17:53.050 align:middle line:90%
It seems completely opposite.

00:17:53.050 --> 00:17:55.480 align:middle line:84%
Because I just said
Fiat-Shamir in contradictory

00:17:55.480 --> 00:17:56.280 align:middle line:90%
of zero knowledge.

00:17:56.280 --> 00:17:58.030 align:middle line:84%
And now, I'm saying
you apply Fiat-Shamir,

00:17:58.030 --> 00:18:00.950 align:middle line:90%
and you get zero knowledge.

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:05.480 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what we get is
noninteractive zero knowledge.

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:09.830 align:middle line:84%
And noninteractive zero
knowledge, the simulator,

00:18:09.830 --> 00:18:11.070 align:middle line:90%
I didn't define what it is.

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:11.612 align:middle line:90%
So it's hard.

00:18:11.612 --> 00:18:12.960 align:middle line:90%
I'll explain it as I go.

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:18.770 align:middle line:84%
But the simulator has more
power than the cheating prover.

00:18:18.770 --> 00:18:21.020 align:middle line:84%
Remember, the
reason I said that,

00:18:21.020 --> 00:18:23.070 align:middle line:84%
if you have a zero
knowledge protocol,

00:18:23.070 --> 00:18:24.710 align:middle line:90%
why can't you apply Fiat-Shamir?

00:18:24.710 --> 00:18:29.630 align:middle line:84%
Because we said, look,
a cheating verifier

00:18:29.630 --> 00:18:31.410 align:middle line:84%
can use the
Fiat-Shamir function.

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:32.550 align:middle line:90%
He can behave maliciously.

00:18:32.550 --> 00:18:34.430 align:middle line:84%
He's behaving like a
Fiat-Shamir function.

00:18:34.430 --> 00:18:37.750 align:middle line:84%
Now, you should be able
to simulate the view.

00:18:37.750 --> 00:18:39.790 align:middle line:84%
So if you have an
efficient simulator that

00:18:39.790 --> 00:18:43.778 align:middle line:84%
simulates the view
for x in the language,

00:18:43.778 --> 00:18:44.820 align:middle line:90%
he's efficient simulator.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:46.990 align:middle line:84%
He doesn't know which
instances are in the language

00:18:46.990 --> 00:18:47.725 align:middle line:90%
or outside the language.

00:18:47.725 --> 00:18:48.225 align:middle line:90%
It's NP.

00:18:48.225 --> 00:18:49.520 align:middle line:90%
It's hard languages.

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:53.170 align:middle line:84%
So he probably will succeed in
simulating the view of instances

00:18:53.170 --> 00:18:54.090 align:middle line:90%
outside the language.

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:58.030 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, he's like a P.
he's a kind of distinguisher.

00:18:58.030 --> 00:19:01.010 align:middle line:84%
You can use him to show that the
language isn't in P, not in NP.

00:19:01.010 --> 00:19:03.700 align:middle line:90%


00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:05.950 align:middle line:84%
And that's why there
cannot be a simulator.

00:19:05.950 --> 00:19:10.390 align:middle line:84%
Because, the simulator, he can
act like a cheating prover.

00:19:10.390 --> 00:19:13.090 align:middle line:84%
In the noninteractive
setting, he cannot.

00:19:13.090 --> 00:19:15.400 align:middle line:84%
Because we're giving the
simulator extra power

00:19:15.400 --> 00:19:17.547 align:middle line:84%
to choose the common
reference string.

00:19:17.547 --> 00:19:19.130 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk about it
when we get there.

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:21.340 align:middle line:84%
But it's a model that's
a little different.

00:19:21.340 --> 00:19:24.880 align:middle line:84%
So actually, I planned
I'm going to talk

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:27.170 align:middle line:84%
about this more in
length when I get there.

00:19:27.170 --> 00:19:29.600 align:middle line:84%
But since you asked, I
want to give a preview.

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:31.075 align:middle line:90%
So indeed, there's tension.

00:19:31.075 --> 00:19:32.450 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see how
to overcome it.

00:19:32.450 --> 00:19:37.750 align:middle line:84%
It's actually really
interesting that it's a belief

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:40.070 align:middle line:90%
that you can't use Fiat-Shamir.

00:19:40.070 --> 00:19:43.110 align:middle line:84%
One, zero knowledge is a kind of
in contradiction to Fiat-Shamir.

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:44.860 align:middle line:84%
But actually, we use
Fiat-Shamir to get

00:19:44.860 --> 00:19:47.680 align:middle line:84%
the noninteractive
version of zero knowledge.

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:50.530 align:middle line:84%
But we'll go into detail
when we get there.

00:19:50.530 --> 00:19:51.910 align:middle line:90%
Great question.

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:55.640 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:19:55.640 --> 00:19:58.910 align:middle line:84%
So my plan next is to
prove the soundness

00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:00.770 align:middle line:84%
of this protocol,
the parallel repeated

00:20:00.770 --> 00:20:02.500 align:middle line:90%
version under Fiat-Shamir.

00:20:02.500 --> 00:20:04.690 align:middle line:84%
But any question
before we go there?

00:20:04.690 --> 00:20:07.520 align:middle line:90%


00:20:07.520 --> 00:20:12.300 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let's-- the
result. So as I said,

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:20.030 align:middle line:84%
this is a result of a Canetti,
Lombardi, and Wichs from 2019.

00:20:20.030 --> 00:20:22.850 align:middle line:90%
It's so beautiful and simple.

00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:26.210 align:middle line:84%
So this was an open question
for a very long time.

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:30.680 align:middle line:84%
And I want to give it to
you as a kind of evidence

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:32.720 align:middle line:84%
or an example of
something that we thought

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:34.710 align:middle line:90%
was so hard, so hard, so hard.

00:20:34.710 --> 00:20:36.680 align:middle line:84%
And then if you
just think about it

00:20:36.680 --> 00:20:39.270 align:middle line:84%
like a little differently,
you twist a little bit,

00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:40.410 align:middle line:90%
it's so easy.

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:44.130 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to have
you guys try to come up

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:45.610 align:middle line:90%
with a proof of this.

00:20:45.610 --> 00:20:47.440 align:middle line:84%
So let's try to do
it interactively.

00:20:47.440 --> 00:20:50.820 align:middle line:84%
And I want you to prove to
me that this protocol P, V--

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:55.300 align:middle line:84%
but think of it, again,
repeated in parallel-- is sound.

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:58.740 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to give
you a few hints,

00:20:58.740 --> 00:21:00.350 align:middle line:90%
and we'll get there together.

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:03.390 align:middle line:84%
So one thing that's really
nice about this protocol

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:06.645 align:middle line:90%
is I want to argue--

00:21:06.645 --> 00:21:09.560 align:middle line:90%


00:21:09.560 --> 00:21:12.640 align:middle line:84%
let's recall, what is
the Fiat-Shamir paradigm?

00:21:12.640 --> 00:21:16.940 align:middle line:84%
The Fiat-Shamir paradigm
says that we choose some hash

00:21:16.940 --> 00:21:21.170 align:middle line:90%
function, some hash key, from--

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:22.940 align:middle line:84%
there's some Fiat-Shamir
hash function

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:24.410 align:middle line:90%
that generates a hash key.

00:21:24.410 --> 00:21:27.890 align:middle line:84%
And now, we want to say,
let me denote for simplicity

00:21:27.890 --> 00:21:32.910 align:middle line:84%
these messages by
alpha, beta and gamma.

00:21:32.910 --> 00:21:37.190 align:middle line:84%
Even for now, let's even not
think of this specific protocol.

00:21:37.190 --> 00:21:38.640 align:middle line:90%
Let's think of just a protocol.

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:44.820 align:middle line:84%
The prover sends alpha, verifier
sends beta, prover sends gamma.

00:21:44.820 --> 00:21:47.690 align:middle line:84%
Let's not even go into
the specific details.

00:21:47.690 --> 00:21:51.142 align:middle line:84%
I want to prove to come
up with a hash function.

00:21:51.142 --> 00:21:52.850 align:middle line:84%
So I want to come up
with a hash function

00:21:52.850 --> 00:21:58.230 align:middle line:84%
h, which generates a key for
a hash and then evaluates.

00:21:58.230 --> 00:22:03.820 align:middle line:84%
That's just a hash function
such that this remains sound.

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:04.810 align:middle line:90%
OK, so I have this.

00:22:04.810 --> 00:22:05.940 align:middle line:90%
There's some NP language x.

00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:08.107 align:middle line:84%
I want to prove whether
it's in the language or not.

00:22:08.107 --> 00:22:09.930 align:middle line:90%
This is my proof system.

00:22:09.930 --> 00:22:12.935 align:middle line:84%
And I want to find a hash
function for which it's sound.

00:22:12.935 --> 00:22:17.060 align:middle line:90%


00:22:17.060 --> 00:22:19.030 align:middle line:84%
At first, it seems like
it should be very easy.

00:22:19.030 --> 00:22:20.590 align:middle line:90%
Why should it be very easy?

00:22:20.590 --> 00:22:23.728 align:middle line:84%
Look, take, let's say,
x not in the language.

00:22:23.728 --> 00:22:26.270 align:middle line:84%
We need to focus on x not in
the language. x in the language,

00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:27.080 align:middle line:90%
it's complete.

00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:28.290 align:middle line:90%
Completeness, that's easy.

00:22:28.290 --> 00:22:30.120 align:middle line:84%
I want to argue
you cannot cheat.

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:32.480 align:middle line:90%
So now, what do I need to say?

00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:34.350 align:middle line:84%
I want that, for any
x in the language--

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:36.260 align:middle line:90%
so fix 1x on the language--

00:22:36.260 --> 00:22:42.260 align:middle line:84%
I want to argue that you
cannot find an alpha and beta,

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:46.810 align:middle line:84%
which is h of alpha, and
the gamma that's accepting.

00:22:46.810 --> 00:22:49.030 align:middle line:90%
But now, this is a proof system.

00:22:49.030 --> 00:22:52.480 align:middle line:84%
So every alpha
has very few betas

00:22:52.480 --> 00:22:56.290 align:middle line:84%
for which there even exists
a gamma that's accepting.

00:22:56.290 --> 00:22:59.870 align:middle line:84%
So again, because it's
fixed x on the language,

00:22:59.870 --> 00:23:02.990 align:middle line:84%
first thing we know is just
by definition of a proof

00:23:02.990 --> 00:23:06.230 align:middle line:84%
that, for every alpha,
any possible alpha,

00:23:06.230 --> 00:23:09.530 align:middle line:84%
there exists very few
betas for which there

00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:11.760 align:middle line:90%
exists an accepting answer.

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:17.490 align:middle line:84%
So there exists few,
very few, betas such

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:25.770 align:middle line:84%
that there exists gamma for
which alpha, beta, gamma

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:26.675 align:middle line:90%
are accepted.

00:23:26.675 --> 00:23:30.050 align:middle line:90%


00:23:30.050 --> 00:23:31.580 align:middle line:84%
So that's the first
thing we know.

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:35.780 align:middle line:84%
Because it's a proof system, by
the virtue of a proof system,

00:23:35.780 --> 00:23:38.720 align:middle line:84%
for every alpha, there
are very, very few.

00:23:38.720 --> 00:23:42.200 align:middle line:84%
Actually, note, in this protocol
specifically, there's one.

00:23:42.200 --> 00:23:44.250 align:middle line:90%
One.

00:23:44.250 --> 00:23:51.140 align:middle line:84%
Because if the graph g does
not have a Hamiltonian cycle,

00:23:51.140 --> 00:23:53.900 align:middle line:84%
take a cheating prover
that sends alpha.

00:23:53.900 --> 00:23:56.100 align:middle line:90%
Alpha is a bunch of commitments.

00:23:56.100 --> 00:23:58.440 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's act in
each commitment.

00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:04.177 align:middle line:84%
Look, either they're setting
a Hamiltonian cycle or not.

00:24:04.177 --> 00:24:05.510 align:middle line:90%
You can't answer both questions.

00:24:05.510 --> 00:24:08.270 align:middle line:84%
We know that, if you can
answer both questions, well,

00:24:08.270 --> 00:24:09.050 align:middle line:90%
it won't be sound.

00:24:09.050 --> 00:24:10.550 align:middle line:84%
So you can only
answer one question.

00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:12.710 align:middle line:84%
And each one can
only answer one.

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:15.750 align:middle line:84%
So this protocol, even for
this specific protocol,

00:24:15.750 --> 00:24:17.930 align:middle line:90%
let's assume even.

00:24:17.930 --> 00:24:22.370 align:middle line:84%
Let's even assume
what any proof tells

00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:24.840 align:middle line:84%
you is that, for every
alpha, there exists a few.

00:24:24.840 --> 00:24:26.900 align:middle line:84%
But for simplicity,
because that's

00:24:26.900 --> 00:24:30.400 align:middle line:84%
the protocol we're
dealing with, let's

00:24:30.400 --> 00:24:35.020 align:middle line:90%
suppose there exists a unique--

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:36.730 align:middle line:84%
this is a symbol for
unique, by the way.

00:24:36.730 --> 00:24:40.005 align:middle line:84%
There exists a unique
beta that can be extended.

00:24:40.005 --> 00:24:43.250 align:middle line:90%


00:24:43.250 --> 00:24:47.460 align:middle line:84%
Now, we need to do
is not hit that beta.

00:24:47.460 --> 00:24:49.110 align:middle line:90%
So all we need--

00:24:49.110 --> 00:24:50.080 align:middle line:90%
I mean, think about it.

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:51.080 align:middle line:90%
It seems so easy.

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:55.420 align:middle line:84%
All we need is to find a
hash h, like a hash key,

00:24:55.420 --> 00:25:04.500 align:middle line:84%
such that even of hash
key in alpha is not equal.

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:06.850 align:middle line:84%
For every alpha there
exists a single bit,

00:25:06.850 --> 00:25:09.060 align:middle line:90%
let me call it beta bad.

00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:12.330 align:middle line:90%
That's a beta we want to avoid.

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:17.280 align:middle line:84%
OK, so for every alpha,
there exists a single beta

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:17.958 align:middle line:90%
bad of alpha.

00:25:17.958 --> 00:25:19.000 align:middle line:90%
It's a function of alpha.

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:20.670 align:middle line:84%
For every alpha, for
every commitment,

00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:22.260 align:middle line:90%
there's a different--

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:23.890 align:middle line:90%
either you can answer 0 or 1.

00:25:23.890 --> 00:25:28.690 align:middle line:84%
So for any alpha, there
exists one challenge, beta,

00:25:28.690 --> 00:25:30.320 align:middle line:90%
for which the prover can cheat.

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:33.220 align:middle line:84%
Now, the cheating prover
wants to hit that bad beta

00:25:33.220 --> 00:25:36.010 align:middle line:84%
because that's what
he can cheat on.

00:25:36.010 --> 00:25:40.450 align:middle line:84%
Our job of protecting
this is to make sure

00:25:40.450 --> 00:25:44.210 align:middle line:84%
that every alpha does not
equal to this beta bad.

00:25:44.210 --> 00:25:46.850 align:middle line:90%


00:25:46.850 --> 00:25:48.300 align:middle line:90%
But how hard can it be?

00:25:48.300 --> 00:25:51.060 align:middle line:90%
We just want to avoid one.

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:52.680 align:middle line:90%
You know what?

00:25:52.680 --> 00:25:55.590 align:middle line:90%
Here, let me give a suggestion.

00:25:55.590 --> 00:25:58.860 align:middle line:90%
Eval hash key or--

00:25:58.860 --> 00:26:08.040 align:middle line:84%
here, h of alpha equals
beta bad of alpha plus 1.

00:26:08.040 --> 00:26:09.520 align:middle line:90%
It's not beta bad.

00:26:09.520 --> 00:26:10.605 align:middle line:90%
I avoid the bad one.

00:26:10.605 --> 00:26:14.763 align:middle line:90%


00:26:14.763 --> 00:26:16.555 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to use this
for my hash function.

00:26:16.555 --> 00:26:20.880 align:middle line:90%


00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:23.120 align:middle line:90%
Anybody see a problem with this?

00:26:23.120 --> 00:26:28.630 align:middle line:90%


00:26:28.630 --> 00:26:30.007 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:26:30.007 --> 00:26:31.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, it's
not really unique

00:26:31.590 --> 00:26:36.130 align:middle line:84%
because you're using the
computational assumption

00:26:36.130 --> 00:26:37.340 align:middle line:90%
for the commitments.

00:26:37.340 --> 00:26:40.190 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: No, actually,
I'm using the computation.

00:26:40.190 --> 00:26:40.780 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:26:40.780 --> 00:26:44.260 align:middle line:84%
So we'll say, well,
actually, it's not unique

00:26:44.260 --> 00:26:46.940 align:middle line:84%
because the commitment here
has a computational assumption.

00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:50.750 align:middle line:84%
But, actually, this commitment
will be statistically binding.

00:26:50.750 --> 00:26:53.590 align:middle line:84%
So it's only the hiding,
the zero knowledge,

00:26:53.590 --> 00:26:54.910 align:middle line:90%
is computational.

00:26:54.910 --> 00:26:56.390 align:middle line:90%
But actually, you can open it.

00:26:56.390 --> 00:26:58.610 align:middle line:84%
Suppose there's really
like you're binded.

00:26:58.610 --> 00:27:01.930 align:middle line:84%
You can really only
open it in one--

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:03.500 align:middle line:84%
you can't open it
two different ways.

00:27:03.500 --> 00:27:05.740 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But the
pigeonhole principle

00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:10.255 align:middle line:84%
is going to be many
conditions for one.

00:27:10.255 --> 00:27:11.380 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: That's fine.

00:27:11.380 --> 00:27:11.920 align:middle line:90%
That's fine.

00:27:11.920 --> 00:27:13.870 align:middle line:84%
But you're right, there's
many permutations.

00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:15.600 align:middle line:84%
But suppose you
can open to both.

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:19.700 align:middle line:84%
Then you can give me both
this message and this message.

00:27:19.700 --> 00:27:21.690 align:middle line:84%
If you give me both
this and both this,

00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:29.160 align:middle line:84%
it means that this
g must have a cycle.

00:27:29.160 --> 00:27:32.560 align:middle line:84%
Because I know that
there's a cycle.

00:27:32.560 --> 00:27:33.480 align:middle line:90%
And guess what?

00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:35.830 align:middle line:90%
It's not in the nonedges.

00:27:35.830 --> 00:27:38.630 align:middle line:84%
So the cycle has
to be in the edges.

00:27:38.630 --> 00:27:43.260 align:middle line:90%
So you can open only one.

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:47.370 align:middle line:84%
So again, all I want,
it seems so easy.

00:27:47.370 --> 00:27:49.190 align:middle line:90%
I have a proof system.

00:27:49.190 --> 00:27:51.530 align:middle line:84%
For every alpha,
actually, the prover

00:27:51.530 --> 00:27:55.080 align:middle line:84%
can only cheat on
one single data.

00:27:55.080 --> 00:27:58.210 align:middle line:84%
All I want to do
is avoid that beta.

00:27:58.210 --> 00:28:00.220 align:middle line:90%
So OK, I'll just avoid that.

00:28:00.220 --> 00:28:01.080 align:middle line:90%
Do beta plus 1.

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:05.330 align:middle line:90%


00:28:05.330 --> 00:28:06.390 align:middle line:90%
This is delicate.

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:10.670 align:middle line:84%
Anybody see why
this doesn't work?

00:28:10.670 --> 00:28:11.170 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:28:11.170 --> 00:28:13.840 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Don't you have to
open the commitment to find out

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:15.372 align:middle line:90%
which is the bad beta?

00:28:15.372 --> 00:28:16.330 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Exactly.

00:28:16.330 --> 00:28:19.990 align:middle line:84%
The problem is computing
beta bad is really hard.

00:28:19.990 --> 00:28:22.090 align:middle line:84%
How do you know what
beta-- for example,

00:28:22.090 --> 00:28:23.660 align:middle line:90%
so computing this is hard.

00:28:23.660 --> 00:28:26.890 align:middle line:84%
Actually, if it were easy,
you wouldn't need interaction

00:28:26.890 --> 00:28:27.760 align:middle line:90%
at all.

00:28:27.760 --> 00:28:28.720 align:middle line:90%
It would be NP.

00:28:28.720 --> 00:28:30.380 align:middle line:84%
You'd have a succinct
witness for NP.

00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:32.836 align:middle line:90%
You just give alpha.

00:28:32.836 --> 00:28:33.800 align:middle line:90%
You compute.

00:28:33.800 --> 00:28:35.860 align:middle line:84%
This is the beta, and
then you give gamma.

00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:38.310 align:middle line:84%
And everything is
information theoretic.

00:28:38.310 --> 00:28:40.210 align:middle line:90%
The problem is computing.

00:28:40.210 --> 00:28:44.190 align:middle line:90%


00:28:44.190 --> 00:28:47.550 align:middle line:84%
The power of this
interaction lies in the fact

00:28:47.550 --> 00:28:51.670 align:middle line:84%
that computing what the
bad beta is is hard.

00:28:51.670 --> 00:28:53.890 align:middle line:84%
Here, for example,
how do you know

00:28:53.890 --> 00:28:56.290 align:middle line:90%
which beta you can cheat on?

00:28:56.290 --> 00:29:01.870 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you open the
commitment, then you see, OK,

00:29:01.870 --> 00:29:02.890 align:middle line:90%
is there a lot of 1s?

00:29:02.890 --> 00:29:04.540 align:middle line:90%
Is it only in cycle?

00:29:04.540 --> 00:29:07.340 align:middle line:84%
But you need to open
this commitment.

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:14.500 align:middle line:84%
So computing this beta is hard
without additional information.

00:29:14.500 --> 00:29:16.380 align:middle line:90%
So now, what do we do?

00:29:16.380 --> 00:29:18.600 align:middle line:84%
So we can't give that as a
hash function because this

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:21.410 align:middle line:90%
is a hard computation.

00:29:21.410 --> 00:29:27.820 align:middle line:90%
So here is the first kind of--

00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:30.710 align:middle line:84%
so there are a few a-ha
moments in this paper.

00:29:30.710 --> 00:29:33.340 align:middle line:90%
The first one is, you know what?

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:37.800 align:middle line:84%
Maybe if this commitment
had some trapdoor,

00:29:37.800 --> 00:29:40.640 align:middle line:84%
maybe there's some
trapdoor information

00:29:40.640 --> 00:29:43.590 align:middle line:84%
that, with the trapdoor,
it becomes easy.

00:29:43.590 --> 00:29:46.210 align:middle line:90%
So then let's do that.

00:29:46.210 --> 00:29:50.650 align:middle line:84%
Let's use trapdoor
in our hash function.

00:29:50.650 --> 00:29:52.480 align:middle line:84%
So now, two things
I'm going to do next--

00:29:52.480 --> 00:29:54.450 align:middle line:84%
first, show you how you
construct a commitment

00:29:54.450 --> 00:29:55.440 align:middle line:90%
with a trapdoor.

00:29:55.440 --> 00:29:57.540 align:middle line:84%
That will also be
useful here, so it's

00:29:57.540 --> 00:29:59.278 align:middle line:90%
going to be a stepping stone.

00:29:59.278 --> 00:30:00.820 align:middle line:84%
So I'll show you
specific commitment,

00:30:00.820 --> 00:30:06.960 align:middle line:84%
which is what we use, which has
a trapdoor to make beta easy.

00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:09.750 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to deal
with the next issue that

00:30:09.750 --> 00:30:13.030 align:middle line:84%
comes up, which the next
a-ha moment appears in,

00:30:13.030 --> 00:30:16.080 align:middle line:84%
which is, well, you can't use
this trapdoor in the clear.

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:23.290 align:middle line:84%
Because you can't give the
cheating people the trapdoor.

00:30:23.290 --> 00:30:25.540 align:middle line:84%
Actually, the problem will
not be the cheating prover.

00:30:25.540 --> 00:30:27.123 align:middle line:84%
The problem, if you
give the trapdoor,

00:30:27.123 --> 00:30:28.720 align:middle line:90%
it's not zero knowledge anymore.

00:30:28.720 --> 00:30:31.120 align:middle line:84%
So this trapdoor
needs to stay hidden.

00:30:31.120 --> 00:30:33.742 align:middle line:90%
You can't just use it.

00:30:33.742 --> 00:30:36.630 align:middle line:84%
The reason it's a
commitment, it has a purpose.

00:30:36.630 --> 00:30:39.000 align:middle line:84%
We don't put things in
the clear for a reason.

00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:44.770 align:middle line:84%
So we'll first see you can make
it efficient given the trapdoor.

00:30:44.770 --> 00:30:48.610 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll show
how this, nevertheless,

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:51.310 align:middle line:84%
even though we need to
keep this trapdoor secret,

00:30:51.310 --> 00:30:53.880 align:middle line:84%
the mere fact that it
exists can be useful

00:30:53.880 --> 00:30:58.380 align:middle line:90%
for us to do the Fiat-Shamir.

00:30:58.380 --> 00:31:00.860 align:middle line:90%
OK, questions?

00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:03.920 align:middle line:84%
Let me remind you that
I really like questions.

00:31:03.920 --> 00:31:07.140 align:middle line:84%
Good, bad, all of them,
they're all fantastic.

00:31:07.140 --> 00:31:07.640 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:31:07.640 --> 00:31:08.150 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I'm just curious.

00:31:08.150 --> 00:31:10.260 align:middle line:84%
So the common random
string, how did you

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:15.590 align:middle line:84%
use to get step where you want
to make the trapdoor somehow

00:31:15.590 --> 00:31:17.420 align:middle line:84%
random enough that
it's not known?

00:31:17.420 --> 00:31:19.340 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: So the
common random string

00:31:19.340 --> 00:31:21.098 align:middle line:90%
will be used in many places.

00:31:21.098 --> 00:31:22.640 align:middle line:84%
The first place it's
going to be used

00:31:22.640 --> 00:31:26.000 align:middle line:84%
is even to make sure my
commitment has a trapdoor.

00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:28.052 align:middle line:84%
And then it's going to
be used again and again.

00:31:28.052 --> 00:31:29.760 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be used
all over the place.

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:32.280 align:middle line:90%
So we'll see that next.

00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:36.900 align:middle line:84%
So any other questions
before we proceed?

00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:39.120 align:middle line:84%
So step one, let
me just tell you

00:31:39.120 --> 00:31:42.360 align:middle line:84%
how to construct the commitment
so that it has a trapdoor.

00:31:42.360 --> 00:31:47.100 align:middle line:84%
And the way I'm going to do it
is just using encryption scheme.

00:31:47.100 --> 00:31:50.790 align:middle line:84%
So let me define the notion
of public key encryption.

00:31:50.790 --> 00:31:53.590 align:middle line:84%
And did we define public
key encryption last time?

00:31:53.590 --> 00:31:57.710 align:middle line:90%


00:31:57.710 --> 00:31:59.680 align:middle line:90%
No, right?

00:31:59.680 --> 00:32:01.430 align:middle line:84%
So let me define the
notion of encryption,

00:32:01.430 --> 00:32:03.590 align:middle line:84%
and we'll use that as
our commitment scheme.

00:32:03.590 --> 00:32:06.920 align:middle line:90%
So what is an encryption?

00:32:06.920 --> 00:32:16.740 align:middle line:84%
So a public key encryption
with message space,

00:32:16.740 --> 00:32:18.710 align:middle line:84%
there's a message space
associated with it--

00:32:18.710 --> 00:32:23.603 align:middle line:90%


00:32:23.603 --> 00:32:25.770 align:middle line:84%
these are the messages that
we're going to encrypt--

00:32:25.770 --> 00:32:32.900 align:middle line:90%
it consists of three algorithms.

00:32:32.900 --> 00:32:38.800 align:middle line:84%
So one is gen. It has its
own key generation not

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:40.550 align:middle line:84%
to be confused with
the hash function that

00:32:40.550 --> 00:32:41.630 align:middle line:90%
generates hash key.

00:32:41.630 --> 00:32:45.710 align:middle line:84%
This generates public
key and secret key.

00:32:45.710 --> 00:32:54.870 align:middle line:84%
So this just generates a pair,
a public key and a secret key.

00:32:54.870 --> 00:33:00.490 align:middle line:84%
Let me just mention
three PPT algorithms.

00:33:00.490 --> 00:33:03.520 align:middle line:84%
So we have Probabilistic
Polynomial Time algorithms.

00:33:03.520 --> 00:33:05.670 align:middle line:84%
The first just generates
two keys, a public key

00:33:05.670 --> 00:33:08.050 align:middle line:90%
and a secret key.

00:33:08.050 --> 00:33:11.560 align:middle line:84%
The encryption algorithm
takes, as input, a public key

00:33:11.560 --> 00:33:14.180 align:middle line:84%
and a message from
the message space.

00:33:14.180 --> 00:33:20.970 align:middle line:84%
And it generates a ciphertext
from a ciphertext space.

00:33:20.970 --> 00:33:22.800 align:middle line:84%
And the third
algorithm decrypts.

00:33:22.800 --> 00:33:27.830 align:middle line:84%
So it takes a secret
key and the ciphertext,

00:33:27.830 --> 00:33:33.710 align:middle line:84%
and it generates a message in
the message space or like that.

00:33:33.710 --> 00:33:34.440 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I failed.

00:33:34.440 --> 00:33:38.090 align:middle line:84%
I tried to decrypt,
but I failed.

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:42.680 align:middle line:84%
And the properties we want,
the first is correctness

00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:47.940 align:middle line:84%
or completeness, which just
says, if you encrypt the message

00:33:47.940 --> 00:33:50.710 align:middle line:84%
and then you decrypt it,
you get your message back.

00:33:50.710 --> 00:33:58.140 align:middle line:84%
So it just says, for every m,
for any message, the probability

00:33:58.140 --> 00:34:00.150 align:middle line:84%
that you decrypt--
so the probability is

00:34:00.150 --> 00:34:12.770 align:middle line:84%
over a sample public key
and secret key from key Gen.

00:34:12.770 --> 00:34:18.924 align:middle line:84%
And then use the secret key
to decrypt an encryption of m.

00:34:18.924 --> 00:34:25.370 align:middle line:90%


00:34:25.370 --> 00:34:30.080 align:middle line:84%
So it says, if you encrypt m,
you generate a pair of keys,

00:34:30.080 --> 00:34:34.250 align:middle line:84%
you encrypt the message m
and then you decrypt it,

00:34:34.250 --> 00:34:38.750 align:middle line:84%
you get back the message
with probability 1.

00:34:38.750 --> 00:34:40.889 align:middle line:84%
That's just correctness,
what you expect.

00:34:40.889 --> 00:34:43.130 align:middle line:84%
If you take a message,
you encrypt it,

00:34:43.130 --> 00:34:48.199 align:middle line:84%
and then you decrypt it, you
should get the message back.

00:34:48.199 --> 00:34:52.070 align:middle line:84%
The important property
is security or what's

00:34:52.070 --> 00:34:53.790 align:middle line:90%
called semantic security.

00:34:53.790 --> 00:35:00.400 align:middle line:90%


00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:03.560 align:middle line:84%
And this says that
any two messages,

00:35:03.560 --> 00:35:06.290 align:middle line:84%
if you encrypt one
or encrypt the other,

00:35:06.290 --> 00:35:07.740 align:middle line:90%
can distinguish between the two.

00:35:07.740 --> 00:35:08.610 align:middle line:90%
They look the same.

00:35:08.610 --> 00:35:10.910 align:middle line:84%
They're computationally
indistinguishable.

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:18.500 align:middle line:84%
So it just says, for every m0
and m1 in the message space--

00:35:18.500 --> 00:35:20.190 align:middle line:90%
so typically, we think--

00:35:20.190 --> 00:35:23.013 align:middle line:84%
by the way, the length
of m is revealed.

00:35:23.013 --> 00:35:24.680 align:middle line:84%
Typically, we think
of the message space

00:35:24.680 --> 00:35:26.660 align:middle line:90%
as having fixed size.

00:35:26.660 --> 00:35:30.900 align:middle line:84%
For us, throughout this entire
lecture and often is the case,

00:35:30.900 --> 00:35:34.460 align:middle line:84%
we can think of m as just
containing two messages, the 0

00:35:34.460 --> 00:35:36.140 align:middle line:90%
message or the 1 message.

00:35:36.140 --> 00:35:37.890 align:middle line:84%
And if you want to
encrypt longer message,

00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:39.660 align:middle line:84%
you just encrypt
bit by bit by bit.

00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:41.740 align:middle line:84%
That's a common way
to do encryption.

00:35:41.740 --> 00:35:43.213 align:middle line:90%
Not in practice, but in theory.

00:35:43.213 --> 00:35:44.880 align:middle line:84%
In practice, it's
often not fast enough.

00:35:44.880 --> 00:35:47.990 align:middle line:84%
But in theory, you can just
encrypt each bit separately.

00:35:47.990 --> 00:35:52.040 align:middle line:84%
But in general, for any two
messages, the message space,

00:35:52.040 --> 00:35:55.500 align:middle line:84%
if it's just 0, 1s, for the
message 0 and for the message 1,

00:35:55.500 --> 00:35:58.940 align:middle line:84%
the claim is that, if you
generate public key according

00:35:58.940 --> 00:36:06.620 align:middle line:84%
to Gen and encrypt m0,
this is indistinguishable,

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:08.390 align:middle line:84%
computationally
indistinguishable,

00:36:08.390 --> 00:36:15.260 align:middle line:84%
from generating a public key
according to Gen and encrypting

00:36:15.260 --> 00:36:17.510 align:middle line:90%
message 1.

00:36:17.510 --> 00:36:19.970 align:middle line:84%
So for any messages,
you know the messages.

00:36:19.970 --> 00:36:20.810 align:middle line:90%
I'm telling you.

00:36:20.810 --> 00:36:24.150 align:middle line:90%
I'm encrypting m0 or m1.

00:36:24.150 --> 00:36:25.785 align:middle line:90%
You can't tell the difference.

00:36:25.785 --> 00:36:26.410 align:middle line:90%
You don't know.

00:36:26.410 --> 00:36:27.535 align:middle line:90%
I'm giving you one of them.

00:36:27.535 --> 00:36:30.600 align:middle line:84%
You have no idea if it's m0
encrypted or m1 encrypted.

00:36:30.600 --> 00:36:33.780 align:middle line:84%
Note, there's no randomness
over the messages for any two

00:36:33.780 --> 00:36:36.490 align:middle line:90%
messages, fixed messages.

00:36:36.490 --> 00:36:36.990 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:36:36.990 --> 00:36:38.615 align:middle line:84%
So even you don't
know if I encrypt M--

00:36:38.615 --> 00:36:41.790 align:middle line:84%
if I give you two encryptions
of 0 or I give you encryption

00:36:41.790 --> 00:36:44.152 align:middle line:84%
of 01, can't tell
the difference.

00:36:44.152 --> 00:36:47.110 align:middle line:90%


00:36:47.110 --> 00:36:49.010 align:middle line:90%
So that's the definition.

00:36:49.010 --> 00:36:51.970 align:middle line:84%
And we have these public
key encryption schemes

00:36:51.970 --> 00:36:56.110 align:middle line:84%
and a whole slew of assumptions,
lattice-based assumption,

00:36:56.110 --> 00:36:58.030 align:middle line:84%
not lattice-based,
like discrete log

00:36:58.030 --> 00:37:02.090 align:middle line:84%
that we saw earlier in
the semester and so on.

00:37:02.090 --> 00:37:04.600 align:middle line:84%
But I don't want to talk about
instantiation because this

00:37:04.600 --> 00:37:07.790 align:middle line:90%
is more class on proof systems.

00:37:07.790 --> 00:37:11.090 align:middle line:84%
And I don't want to make
it too hard core crypto.

00:37:11.090 --> 00:37:13.910 align:middle line:84%
So let me just use this-- not
show how to actually construct

00:37:13.910 --> 00:37:17.130 align:middle line:84%
this, but we have many
instantiation constructions.

00:37:17.130 --> 00:37:19.130 align:middle line:90%
But suppose we have this.

00:37:19.130 --> 00:37:22.200 align:middle line:84%
Let me show you what
the commitment would be.

00:37:22.200 --> 00:37:24.290 align:middle line:84%
So now, what I want
the commitment to be,

00:37:24.290 --> 00:37:27.890 align:middle line:84%
instead of commitment,
make this an encryption.

00:37:27.890 --> 00:37:30.545 align:middle line:84%
Let me actually
put another color.

00:37:30.545 --> 00:37:37.060 align:middle line:90%


00:37:37.060 --> 00:37:44.860 align:middle line:84%
So make this be an encryption
with some public key of pi.

00:37:44.860 --> 00:37:48.800 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm telling the prover
now, I'm telling him, look,

00:37:48.800 --> 00:37:51.470 align:middle line:84%
I want to be able in
the analysis at least,

00:37:51.470 --> 00:37:57.100 align:middle line:84%
to open up there and see
where the 1s are and where are

00:37:57.100 --> 00:38:00.310 align:middle line:84%
the 01s, so that later I'll
be able to understand where

00:38:00.310 --> 00:38:01.290 align:middle line:90%
the nonedges are.

00:38:01.290 --> 00:38:04.490 align:middle line:90%
And so what's the bad beta?

00:38:04.490 --> 00:38:08.430 align:middle line:84%
So now, I want to
tell the prover--

00:38:08.430 --> 00:38:11.240 align:middle line:84%
so please, the commitment
I want you to send me

00:38:11.240 --> 00:38:12.860 align:middle line:90%
is the encryption.

00:38:12.860 --> 00:38:13.910 align:middle line:90%
Now, it's hiding.

00:38:13.910 --> 00:38:14.760 align:middle line:90%
It's still hiding.

00:38:14.760 --> 00:38:15.260 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:38:15.260 --> 00:38:17.782 align:middle line:90%


00:38:17.782 --> 00:38:18.990 align:middle line:90%
So it's still zero knowledge.

00:38:18.990 --> 00:38:20.782 align:middle line:84%
It's still satisfies
kind of-- it's locked.

00:38:20.782 --> 00:38:23.590 align:middle line:84%
It's like the verifier has
no idea what's in there.

00:38:23.590 --> 00:38:26.970 align:middle line:90%


00:38:26.970 --> 00:38:28.230 align:middle line:90%
And it's still binding.

00:38:28.230 --> 00:38:32.440 align:middle line:84%
Because once you
encrypt, you're stuck.

00:38:32.440 --> 00:38:35.698 align:middle line:90%


00:38:35.698 --> 00:38:37.740 align:middle line:84%
The message is sitting
there because then you can

00:38:37.740 --> 00:38:39.670 align:middle line:90%
use a secret key to decrypt it.

00:38:39.670 --> 00:38:42.440 align:middle line:84%
So it really has the hiding
and binding property.

00:38:42.440 --> 00:38:44.190 align:middle line:84%
Great, it's like a
real commitment scheme.

00:38:44.190 --> 00:38:48.060 align:middle line:90%


00:38:48.060 --> 00:38:50.410 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:52.445 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:38:52.445 --> 00:38:54.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does
public key encryption

00:38:54.170 --> 00:38:56.870 align:middle line:84%
imply that there's
no possibility

00:38:56.870 --> 00:39:00.703 align:middle line:84%
of a second secret key that
decrypts it to something else?

00:39:00.703 --> 00:39:02.120 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: OK,
great question.

00:39:02.120 --> 00:39:04.580 align:middle line:90%
Great question.

00:39:04.580 --> 00:39:05.180 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:39:05.180 --> 00:39:08.690 align:middle line:84%
So you're asking, wait,
what if the public key has

00:39:08.690 --> 00:39:14.840 align:middle line:84%
many secret keys associated with
it and one of them decrypts to m

00:39:14.840 --> 00:39:17.010 align:middle line:84%
and the other one decrypts
to m prime, let's say?

00:39:17.010 --> 00:39:18.990 align:middle line:90%
So now, here I say, no.

00:39:18.990 --> 00:39:21.120 align:middle line:90%
By my definition, no.

00:39:21.120 --> 00:39:23.880 align:middle line:84%
Because my
correctness says that,

00:39:23.880 --> 00:39:25.740 align:middle line:84%
if you choose public
key and secret key

00:39:25.740 --> 00:39:30.090 align:middle line:84%
pair with probability
1, you will always

00:39:30.090 --> 00:39:31.470 align:middle line:90%
get your message back.

00:39:31.470 --> 00:39:35.110 align:middle line:84%
So there's no secret key that
will give you different message.

00:39:35.110 --> 00:39:36.660 align:middle line:90%
That's my definition.

00:39:36.660 --> 00:39:40.530 align:middle line:84%
So what you're
proposing is not for me.

00:39:40.530 --> 00:39:43.290 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't satisfy
my definition.

00:39:43.290 --> 00:39:49.830 align:middle line:84%
So now, use a public encryption
that satisfies my definition.

00:39:49.830 --> 00:39:51.790 align:middle line:90%
Now, you're binded.

00:39:51.790 --> 00:39:52.290 align:middle line:90%
Good?

00:39:52.290 --> 00:39:53.080 align:middle line:90%
Great question.

00:39:53.080 --> 00:39:54.940 align:middle line:84%
These are really,
really great questions.

00:39:54.940 --> 00:39:56.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:39:56.110 --> 00:39:58.300 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Here in the
probability expression,

00:39:58.300 --> 00:40:00.720 align:middle line:84%
you're choosing public key
as secret key here all coming

00:40:00.720 --> 00:40:02.080 align:middle line:90%
from the same Gen function.

00:40:02.080 --> 00:40:02.913 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Yeah.

00:40:02.913 --> 00:40:04.440 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: But what if--

00:40:04.440 --> 00:40:06.630 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't give you
anything about a different--

00:40:06.630 --> 00:40:08.463 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: OK, very,
very good question,

00:40:08.463 --> 00:40:11.040 align:middle line:90%
very good question.

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:13.980 align:middle line:90%
So the point was the following.

00:40:13.980 --> 00:40:18.000 align:middle line:84%
Look, we have all these
guarantees, the correctness

00:40:18.000 --> 00:40:20.640 align:middle line:84%
and the security,
assuming the public key is

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:23.300 align:middle line:90%
generated according to Gen.

00:40:23.300 --> 00:40:28.840 align:middle line:84%
Now, my question here, who
generates this public key?

00:40:28.840 --> 00:40:30.730 align:middle line:84%
How do I know it generates
according to Gen?

00:40:30.730 --> 00:40:37.760 align:middle line:84%
If we let the prover generate
the public key, then maybe

00:40:37.760 --> 00:40:42.420 align:middle line:84%
it's a bad public key that is
never going to appear here.

00:40:42.420 --> 00:40:46.510 align:middle line:84%
And for that bad public key,
maybe your concern is valid.

00:40:46.510 --> 00:40:49.840 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you can open
it in different ways.

00:40:49.840 --> 00:40:51.740 align:middle line:90%
And you're completely right.

00:40:51.740 --> 00:40:53.260 align:middle line:90%
That is a problem.

00:40:53.260 --> 00:40:57.890 align:middle line:84%
Actually, it's not a
hypothetical problem.

00:40:57.890 --> 00:40:59.910 align:middle line:84%
Our scheme that we use
that we want to get-- we

00:40:59.910 --> 00:41:01.660 align:middle line:84%
want to get this under
lattice assumption.

00:41:01.660 --> 00:41:04.990 align:middle line:84%
That specific scheme we use has
that property, has that problem

00:41:04.990 --> 00:41:08.880 align:middle line:84%
that, if the key is generated
badly, actually, there

00:41:08.880 --> 00:41:09.590 align:middle line:90%
is no binding.

00:41:09.590 --> 00:41:10.090 align:middle line:90%
I can cheat.

00:41:10.090 --> 00:41:11.960 align:middle line:90%
I can open two different ways.

00:41:11.960 --> 00:41:15.200 align:middle line:84%
I will never be able to do that
if I generate according to Gen.

00:41:15.200 --> 00:41:17.720 align:middle line:84%
But if I generated
maliciously, I

00:41:17.720 --> 00:41:19.230 align:middle line:84%
can choose a very
bad public key.

00:41:19.230 --> 00:41:20.640 align:middle line:84%
Nobody will be able to
tell the difference.

00:41:20.640 --> 00:41:22.190 align:middle line:84%
It will look like
a good public key,

00:41:22.190 --> 00:41:25.790 align:middle line:84%
but it's actually generated
completely differently.

00:41:25.790 --> 00:41:27.920 align:middle line:84%
And it would have
never appeared in Gen.

00:41:27.920 --> 00:41:32.020 align:middle line:84%
And with that public key,
there is no correctness.

00:41:32.020 --> 00:41:35.320 align:middle line:84%
And I can cheat and I
can do whatever I want.

00:41:35.320 --> 00:41:40.710 align:middle line:84%
So very good, you found the
problem in this approach.

00:41:40.710 --> 00:41:41.530 align:middle line:90%
So what do we do?

00:41:41.530 --> 00:41:42.610 align:middle line:90%
How do we fix it?

00:41:42.610 --> 00:41:45.500 align:middle line:90%


00:41:45.500 --> 00:41:47.797 align:middle line:84%
So this comes to your
question at the beginning.

00:41:47.797 --> 00:41:48.630 align:middle line:90%
Well, you know what?

00:41:48.630 --> 00:41:51.980 align:middle line:84%
Let's assume that there's a
trusted party that generates

00:41:51.980 --> 00:41:53.870 align:middle line:90%
this public key for us.

00:41:53.870 --> 00:41:56.120 align:middle line:84%
So let's assume, for
now, that there's kind

00:41:56.120 --> 00:41:57.870 align:middle line:90%
of a common reference string.

00:41:57.870 --> 00:42:00.920 align:middle line:84%
We all agree there's a
public key sitting here.

00:42:00.920 --> 00:42:03.980 align:middle line:84%
That's in what's called
a common reference string

00:42:03.980 --> 00:42:05.730 align:middle line:90%
or a common random string.

00:42:05.730 --> 00:42:09.037 align:middle line:84%
Everybody knows it, both
the prover and the verifier.

00:42:09.037 --> 00:42:10.370 align:middle line:90%
We all agree on this public key.

00:42:10.370 --> 00:42:11.900 align:middle line:84%
And we assume that
it was trusted,

00:42:11.900 --> 00:42:16.720 align:middle line:84%
like generated according to
Gen. Nobody has the secret key.

00:42:16.720 --> 00:42:17.740 align:middle line:90%
Nobody has a secret key.

00:42:17.740 --> 00:42:20.160 align:middle line:84%
We'll never need to
open the secret key.

00:42:20.160 --> 00:42:23.380 align:middle line:84%
So in this protocol,
what do I do?

00:42:23.380 --> 00:42:24.360 align:middle line:90%
I'm a prover.

00:42:24.360 --> 00:42:25.710 align:middle line:90%
I encrypt bit by bit.

00:42:25.710 --> 00:42:27.140 align:middle line:90%
So how do I encrypt?

00:42:27.140 --> 00:42:28.890 align:middle line:84%
So I didn't say,
but this encryption

00:42:28.890 --> 00:42:30.340 align:middle line:90%
is a randomized protocol.

00:42:30.340 --> 00:42:33.190 align:middle line:90%
So I choose randomness.

00:42:33.190 --> 00:42:33.940 align:middle line:90%
I encrypt the bit.

00:42:33.940 --> 00:42:34.690 align:middle line:90%
I choose randomness.

00:42:34.690 --> 00:42:35.440 align:middle line:90%
I encrypt the next bit.

00:42:35.440 --> 00:42:36.040 align:middle line:90%
I choose randomness.

00:42:36.040 --> 00:42:37.957 align:middle line:84%
I encrypt the next bit
and so on and so forth.

00:42:37.957 --> 00:42:40.470 align:middle line:84%
And when I'm asked to open,
I reveal my randomness.

00:42:40.470 --> 00:42:41.410 align:middle line:90%
Look, how I encrypted.

00:42:41.410 --> 00:42:42.670 align:middle line:90%
See, there is randomness.

00:42:42.670 --> 00:42:47.160 align:middle line:84%
I took the encryption
with public key and my bit

00:42:47.160 --> 00:42:48.730 align:middle line:90%
0 or 1 with this randomness.

00:42:48.730 --> 00:42:50.410 align:middle line:84%
And see, that's
what's written here.

00:42:50.410 --> 00:42:54.403 align:middle line:84%
So I can just open
without using secret key.

00:42:54.403 --> 00:42:55.570 align:middle line:90%
Nobody knows the secret key.

00:42:55.570 --> 00:42:56.850 align:middle line:90%
The secret key is gone.

00:42:56.850 --> 00:42:59.670 align:middle line:84%
Someone just came
with the public key.

00:42:59.670 --> 00:43:02.890 align:middle line:84%
And I open here not
with the secret key,

00:43:02.890 --> 00:43:06.310 align:middle line:84%
but I show you the randomness
that I used to encrypt.

00:43:06.310 --> 00:43:10.840 align:middle line:84%
And that kind of is
an opening for me.

00:43:10.840 --> 00:43:13.890 align:middle line:90%


00:43:13.890 --> 00:43:14.737 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:43:14.737 --> 00:43:16.320 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does that
mean the CRS can't

00:43:16.320 --> 00:43:19.342 align:middle line:84%
be like a fully
uniformly random string?

00:43:19.342 --> 00:43:21.300 align:middle line:84%
Because if you were to
read out the public key,

00:43:21.300 --> 00:43:24.610 align:middle line:84%
you could also pull
out the secret key?

00:43:24.610 --> 00:43:27.200 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: OK,
great, great question.

00:43:27.200 --> 00:43:30.090 align:middle line:84%
The question was, does that
mean that the CRS here cannot be

00:43:30.090 --> 00:43:30.870 align:middle line:90%
truly random?

00:43:30.870 --> 00:43:32.120 align:middle line:90%
Because it's not truly random.

00:43:32.120 --> 00:43:33.330 align:middle line:90%
It's a public key.

00:43:33.330 --> 00:43:36.260 align:middle line:84%
A public key may
not be truly random.

00:43:36.260 --> 00:43:39.630 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is,
well, first of all,

00:43:39.630 --> 00:43:43.010 align:middle line:84%
some public key happened
to be truly random.

00:43:43.010 --> 00:43:47.370 align:middle line:84%
The public key for this
scheme is not truly random.

00:43:47.370 --> 00:43:52.280 align:middle line:84%
However, it's indistinguishable
from being truly random.

00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:55.170 align:middle line:84%
So the scheme we actually use,
the public key has the property.

00:43:55.170 --> 00:43:55.890 align:middle line:90%
It's not random.

00:43:55.890 --> 00:43:59.090 align:middle line:84%
It's like a LWE for those who
know what learning with error

00:43:59.090 --> 00:43:59.810 align:middle line:90%
is.

00:43:59.810 --> 00:44:02.850 align:middle line:90%
But that's not truly random.

00:44:02.850 --> 00:44:07.860 align:middle line:84%
However, it is
indistinguishable from random.

00:44:07.860 --> 00:44:10.980 align:middle line:84%
Now, if you have soundness
with public key that's

00:44:10.980 --> 00:44:12.840 align:middle line:84%
indistinguishable
from random, soundness

00:44:12.840 --> 00:44:15.420 align:middle line:84%
should hold even if
it's completely random.

00:44:15.420 --> 00:44:18.450 align:middle line:84%
So actually, you can make
this completely random.

00:44:18.450 --> 00:44:21.600 align:middle line:84%
And actually, in the paper, they
actually take advantage of that.

00:44:21.600 --> 00:44:24.640 align:middle line:84%
Because if the public
key is completely random,

00:44:24.640 --> 00:44:26.970 align:middle line:84%
then you get statistical
zero knowledge,

00:44:26.970 --> 00:44:28.320 align:middle line:90%
computational soundness.

00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:31.500 align:middle line:90%


00:44:31.500 --> 00:44:33.480 align:middle line:84%
In this protocol,
especially when

00:44:33.480 --> 00:44:36.520 align:middle line:84%
you go to the noninteractive
version, there's a tension.

00:44:36.520 --> 00:44:39.120 align:middle line:84%
You can either get
statistical soundness

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:42.000 align:middle line:84%
and computational zero knowledge
or statistical zero knowledge

00:44:42.000 --> 00:44:43.300 align:middle line:90%
and computational soundness.

00:44:43.300 --> 00:44:45.060 align:middle line:84%
And you can play
with this public key,

00:44:45.060 --> 00:44:48.232 align:middle line:84%
whether to make it kind of the
true public key in which case

00:44:48.232 --> 00:44:50.190 align:middle line:84%
you get statistical
soundness and computational

00:44:50.190 --> 00:44:51.190 align:middle line:90%
zero knowledge.

00:44:51.190 --> 00:44:55.180 align:middle line:84%
Or if you want to take a fake
public key, which is all 0,

00:44:55.180 --> 00:44:59.110 align:middle line:84%
and then you get computational
soundness and statistical zero

00:44:59.110 --> 00:44:59.610 align:middle line:90%
knowledge.

00:44:59.610 --> 00:45:03.890 align:middle line:84%
So you can play
with these things.

00:45:03.890 --> 00:45:06.800 align:middle line:90%


00:45:06.800 --> 00:45:10.520 align:middle line:84%
So this is the protocol
for which we'll

00:45:10.520 --> 00:45:13.030 align:middle line:90%
show that Fiat-Shamir holds.

00:45:13.030 --> 00:45:15.160 align:middle line:84%
So again, the commitment
is just replaced

00:45:15.160 --> 00:45:19.020 align:middle line:90%
with a public key encryption.

00:45:19.020 --> 00:45:20.460 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:45:20.460 --> 00:45:22.660 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The common
reference string, I mean,

00:45:22.660 --> 00:45:24.670 align:middle line:84%
the public key is
by, for example,

00:45:24.670 --> 00:45:27.000 align:middle line:90%
change is a way of opening.

00:45:27.000 --> 00:45:30.870 align:middle line:84%
For example, we also
require the prover

00:45:30.870 --> 00:45:37.603 align:middle line:84%
to provide the
randomness for Gen.

00:45:37.603 --> 00:45:39.020 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: Oh,
that's a good--

00:45:39.020 --> 00:45:39.720 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:45:39.720 --> 00:45:41.810 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:45:41.810 --> 00:45:42.660 align:middle line:90%
Good suggestion.

00:45:42.660 --> 00:45:44.430 align:middle line:84%
What you're saying
is you're saying,

00:45:44.430 --> 00:45:47.760 align:middle line:84%
wait, how about he
gives this public key.

00:45:47.760 --> 00:45:49.380 align:middle line:90%
It can be malicious.

00:45:49.380 --> 00:45:52.618 align:middle line:84%
But when he opens,
we'll ask him--

00:45:52.618 --> 00:45:54.410 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I'll tell you
the problem with that.

00:45:54.410 --> 00:45:57.230 align:middle line:84%
If, when he opens, he
gives the randomness

00:45:57.230 --> 00:45:58.910 align:middle line:84%
he used-- so you're
suggesting, why

00:45:58.910 --> 00:46:00.410 align:middle line:84%
don't he give the
randomness he used

00:46:00.410 --> 00:46:01.577 align:middle line:90%
to generate this public key?

00:46:01.577 --> 00:46:03.980 align:middle line:84%
And now, you know that
it's a valid public key.

00:46:03.980 --> 00:46:06.240 align:middle line:84%
That will, indeed,
ensure soundness.

00:46:06.240 --> 00:46:09.470 align:middle line:84%
However, that will break
the zero knowledge.

00:46:09.470 --> 00:46:11.220 align:middle line:84%
Because once you
give the randomness,

00:46:11.220 --> 00:46:12.890 align:middle line:84%
you also give the
secret key away.

00:46:12.890 --> 00:46:16.490 align:middle line:84%
Because this randomness was
used to generate both of them.

00:46:16.490 --> 00:46:20.870 align:middle line:84%
And if the secret key, then
you lost the zero knowledge.

00:46:20.870 --> 00:46:21.930 align:middle line:90%
But great suggestion.

00:46:21.930 --> 00:46:22.860 align:middle line:90%
OK, great, you guys.

00:46:22.860 --> 00:46:25.027 align:middle line:84%
It's clear that you're
thinking, which is fantastic.

00:46:25.027 --> 00:46:28.000 align:middle line:90%
Great suggestions.

00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:32.420 align:middle line:84%
Any other questions
before we move on?

00:46:32.420 --> 00:46:36.140 align:middle line:90%


00:46:36.140 --> 00:46:37.690 align:middle line:90%
So we're making progress.

00:46:37.690 --> 00:46:39.080 align:middle line:90%
Now, what do we know?

00:46:39.080 --> 00:46:43.780 align:middle line:84%
So let me hide this
encryption for a second.

00:46:43.780 --> 00:46:46.130 align:middle line:90%
Let's go back here.

00:46:46.130 --> 00:46:50.670 align:middle line:84%
Now, I want you
actually to forget

00:46:50.670 --> 00:46:52.420 align:middle line:84%
that this was an
encryption, because we'll

00:46:52.420 --> 00:46:53.990 align:middle line:90%
use another encryption.

00:46:53.990 --> 00:46:56.385 align:middle line:84%
And I don't want you to be
confused with this encryption,

00:46:56.385 --> 00:46:57.260 align:middle line:90%
the other encryption.

00:46:57.260 --> 00:46:59.560 align:middle line:84%
What I want you to remember
about the commitment,

00:46:59.560 --> 00:47:03.253 align:middle line:84%
that there's some trapdoor,
like a secret key.

00:47:03.253 --> 00:47:05.170 align:middle line:84%
I'll call it trapdoor
on purpose because we'll

00:47:05.170 --> 00:47:08.270 align:middle line:84%
have another encryption
which will have a secret key.

00:47:08.270 --> 00:47:11.620 align:middle line:84%
So what I want you to
remember about this commitment

00:47:11.620 --> 00:47:15.100 align:middle line:84%
is that there's some
trapdoor information

00:47:15.100 --> 00:47:16.840 align:middle line:84%
that, with that
trapdoor information,

00:47:16.840 --> 00:47:20.595 align:middle line:84%
you can open and
know which b is bad.

00:47:20.595 --> 00:47:23.380 align:middle line:90%


00:47:23.380 --> 00:47:30.610 align:middle line:84%
So a trapdoor has a commitment,
has some trapdoor information,

00:47:30.610 --> 00:47:33.720 align:middle line:84%
which allows you to open it, in
that case, open the encryption.

00:47:33.720 --> 00:47:35.550 align:middle line:90%
And then where the 1s are.

00:47:35.550 --> 00:47:43.890 align:middle line:84%
And that will help you
decide where the bad b lies.

00:47:43.890 --> 00:47:47.640 align:middle line:84%
Actually, you know, I think I
need to also add a commitment

00:47:47.640 --> 00:47:49.910 align:middle line:90%
to pi there.

00:47:49.910 --> 00:47:55.930 align:middle line:84%
Because, otherwise,
how do you know which--

00:47:55.930 --> 00:47:57.110 align:middle line:90%
maybe you can tell anyway.

00:47:57.110 --> 00:47:58.902 align:middle line:84%
If you had a commitment
to pi, it's easier.

00:47:58.902 --> 00:48:02.630 align:middle line:84%
But if you don't add
a commitment to pi,

00:48:02.630 --> 00:48:05.940 align:middle line:84%
is it clear that just by
seeing 1s it's easy to test?

00:48:05.940 --> 00:48:19.267 align:middle line:90%


00:48:19.267 --> 00:48:19.850 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, I guess.

00:48:19.850 --> 00:48:21.510 align:middle line:84%
I guess if you open
both, you can see both.

00:48:21.510 --> 00:48:22.843 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, I don't think you need it.

00:48:22.843 --> 00:48:24.610 align:middle line:90%
But anyway-- so, good.

00:48:24.610 --> 00:48:26.180 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:48:26.180 --> 00:48:27.970 align:middle line:84%
So now, what I
want to do, I want

00:48:27.970 --> 00:48:29.960 align:middle line:90%
to say here's my hash function.

00:48:29.960 --> 00:48:33.580 align:middle line:90%
It has trapdoor hardwired to it.

00:48:33.580 --> 00:48:36.500 align:middle line:84%
Once he knows the
trapdoor, it's a trapdoor,

00:48:36.500 --> 00:48:38.530 align:middle line:84%
depends on the public
key and the CRS.

00:48:38.530 --> 00:48:40.330 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't depend
on the actual--

00:48:40.330 --> 00:48:41.830 align:middle line:84%
once he has the
trapdoor, he'll know

00:48:41.830 --> 00:48:43.590 align:middle line:90%
what the bad thing is and done.

00:48:43.590 --> 00:48:47.420 align:middle line:90%


00:48:47.420 --> 00:48:54.930 align:middle line:84%
So one can say, OK, use this as
the Fiat-Shamir hash function.

00:48:54.930 --> 00:48:56.030 align:middle line:90%
Sufficient, it's good.

00:48:56.030 --> 00:48:59.850 align:middle line:84%
However, as we said, the problem
is then you know the trapdoor.

00:48:59.850 --> 00:49:02.440 align:middle line:84%
It's sitting there,
the trapdoor.

00:49:02.440 --> 00:49:05.680 align:middle line:84%
And once you're in the trapdoor,
it's not zero knowledge.

00:49:05.680 --> 00:49:08.170 align:middle line:90%
So that's the problem.

00:49:08.170 --> 00:49:12.740 align:middle line:84%
And then there were
attempts to try to do, OK,

00:49:12.740 --> 00:49:14.620 align:middle line:90%
let's obfuscate these things.

00:49:14.620 --> 00:49:17.590 align:middle line:84%
And a lot of hammers were
thrown in this direction.

00:49:17.590 --> 00:49:19.850 align:middle line:84%
And it resulted in
crazy assumptions.

00:49:19.850 --> 00:49:22.540 align:middle line:84%
And anyway, there was a
long line of work where

00:49:22.540 --> 00:49:23.690 align:middle line:90%
that's what we tried to do.

00:49:23.690 --> 00:49:26.341 align:middle line:90%


00:49:26.341 --> 00:49:30.030 align:middle line:90%
And then here is their idea.

00:49:30.030 --> 00:49:32.320 align:middle line:84%
So now, I can tell you
the next a-ha moment.

00:49:32.320 --> 00:49:35.550 align:middle line:84%
So again, one option
is to throw what's

00:49:35.550 --> 00:49:36.780 align:middle line:90%
called obfuscation at this.

00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:38.322 align:middle line:84%
For those who don't
know obfuscation,

00:49:38.322 --> 00:49:39.930 align:middle line:84%
don't worry about
it-- essentially,

00:49:39.930 --> 00:49:42.930 align:middle line:84%
garbling so that the trapdoor
will be really hidden there.

00:49:42.930 --> 00:49:44.440 align:middle line:90%
Nobody will be able to find it.

00:49:44.440 --> 00:49:47.910 align:middle line:84%
And even then, it was a
messy approach that we tried.

00:49:47.910 --> 00:49:50.070 align:middle line:84%
And we got really, really
strong assumptions,

00:49:50.070 --> 00:49:52.560 align:middle line:84%
which I have no idea if
they were true or not.

00:49:52.560 --> 00:49:55.560 align:middle line:84%
It was kind of a messy
approach that a bunch of papers

00:49:55.560 --> 00:49:58.530 align:middle line:84%
kind of followed that
approach, very complicated.

00:49:58.530 --> 00:50:01.050 align:middle line:90%


00:50:01.050 --> 00:50:02.520 align:middle line:90%
Then came this paper.

00:50:02.520 --> 00:50:08.100 align:middle line:90%
And here is the beautiful idea.

00:50:08.100 --> 00:50:09.970 align:middle line:84%
So the beautiful idea
was the following.

00:50:09.970 --> 00:50:11.590 align:middle line:90%
They said, you know what?

00:50:11.590 --> 00:50:13.400 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, you can't give trapdoor.

00:50:13.400 --> 00:50:14.860 align:middle line:90%
That's a problem.

00:50:14.860 --> 00:50:16.720 align:middle line:90%
Give an encryption of trapdoor.

00:50:16.720 --> 00:50:17.990 align:middle line:90%
Forget about this encryption.

00:50:17.990 --> 00:50:19.340 align:middle line:90%
That's a different encryption.

00:50:19.340 --> 00:50:21.870 align:middle line:84%
So it's now fresh,
new encryption.

00:50:21.870 --> 00:50:23.790 align:middle line:84%
Why don't you give
encryption-- let

00:50:23.790 --> 00:50:26.860 align:middle line:84%
me denote the encryption
of trapdoor here by hat.

00:50:26.860 --> 00:50:30.690 align:middle line:84%
That's a common way we use fully
homomorphic encryption, which

00:50:30.690 --> 00:50:32.530 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to define next.

00:50:32.530 --> 00:50:37.490 align:middle line:84%
So they said put in this
Fiat-Shamir hash function,

00:50:37.490 --> 00:50:39.760 align:middle line:84%
an encryption of
the trapdoor that

00:50:39.760 --> 00:50:42.520 align:middle line:90%
helps you open the commitment.

00:50:42.520 --> 00:50:44.180 align:middle line:90%
That's like, so, OK.

00:50:44.180 --> 00:50:45.307 align:middle line:90%
And then what?

00:50:45.307 --> 00:50:47.140 align:middle line:84%
What are we going to
do with the ciphertext?

00:50:47.140 --> 00:50:48.348 align:middle line:90%
We can't do anything with it.

00:50:48.348 --> 00:50:49.580 align:middle line:90%
Ciphertext is like junk.

00:50:49.580 --> 00:50:51.320 align:middle line:84%
What are we going
to do with that?

00:50:51.320 --> 00:50:53.450 align:middle line:90%
So the idea is you know what?

00:50:53.450 --> 00:50:55.940 align:middle line:90%
Don't just use any encryption.

00:50:55.940 --> 00:51:01.240 align:middle line:84%
Use what's called a fully
homomorphic encryption.

00:51:01.240 --> 00:51:03.290 align:middle line:84%
What is a fully
homomorphic encryption?

00:51:03.290 --> 00:51:04.780 align:middle line:90%
I'll explain that next.

00:51:04.780 --> 00:51:07.180 align:middle line:84%
But essentially,
what it does, it

00:51:07.180 --> 00:51:11.570 align:middle line:84%
allows you to compute
unencrypted data.

00:51:11.570 --> 00:51:14.380 align:middle line:84%
So a fully
homomorphic encryption

00:51:14.380 --> 00:51:20.200 align:middle line:84%
is exactly the same as
an encryption scheme.

00:51:20.200 --> 00:51:22.930 align:middle line:84%
So a fully homomorphic, it's
a public key encryption.

00:51:22.930 --> 00:51:25.630 align:middle line:90%


00:51:25.630 --> 00:51:36.550 align:middle line:84%
So let me write a
fully homomorphic

00:51:36.550 --> 00:51:40.400 align:middle line:84%
public key encryption scheme
is encryption scheme Gen

00:51:40.400 --> 00:51:41.700 align:middle line:90%
encrypt and decrypt.

00:51:41.700 --> 00:51:45.770 align:middle line:84%
But it also allows you to do
computation on encrypted data.

00:51:45.770 --> 00:51:47.290 align:middle line:90%
So what do I mean?

00:51:47.290 --> 00:51:48.860 align:middle line:90%
There is an eval function.

00:51:48.860 --> 00:51:51.690 align:middle line:90%


00:51:51.690 --> 00:51:54.600 align:middle line:84%
It takes a bunch of
ciphertexts, and it

00:51:54.600 --> 00:51:57.420 align:middle line:84%
can do computation
on the bits hiding

00:51:57.420 --> 00:51:59.780 align:middle line:90%
underneath the ciphertext.

00:51:59.780 --> 00:52:07.100 align:middle line:84%
So it takes a public key, a
description of a circuit, c.

00:52:07.100 --> 00:52:10.830 align:middle line:84%
Think of it with additions
and multiplication mod 2.

00:52:10.830 --> 00:52:12.280 align:middle line:90%
And it gets a ciphertext.

00:52:12.280 --> 00:52:16.930 align:middle line:84%
So encryption of b1, bit
1, let me think of it now,

00:52:16.930 --> 00:52:22.250 align:middle line:84%
as I said, think of it as the
message space being 0 and 1.

00:52:22.250 --> 00:52:24.560 align:middle line:90%
So it gets encryption of bits.

00:52:24.560 --> 00:52:28.250 align:middle line:84%
Let's say encryption-- or
I'll just call it ciphertext,

00:52:28.250 --> 00:52:32.840 align:middle line:84%
ciphertext 1 up to
ciphertext n each one

00:52:32.840 --> 00:52:34.860 align:middle line:90%
encrypting a single bit.

00:52:34.860 --> 00:52:40.130 align:middle line:84%
The circuit takes as input
n bits and produces a bit.

00:52:40.130 --> 00:52:43.370 align:middle line:90%
And it output some ciphertext.

00:52:43.370 --> 00:52:46.640 align:middle line:84%
And this ciphertext
if, here, you

00:52:46.640 --> 00:52:50.350 align:middle line:84%
have encryption of
b1, b2 up to bn,

00:52:50.350 --> 00:52:57.650 align:middle line:84%
this ciphertext should have
encryption of c of b1 up to bn.

00:52:57.650 --> 00:53:03.130 align:middle line:84%
So what this eval does, it
gets encryption of bits,

00:53:03.130 --> 00:53:06.560 align:middle line:84%
and it can compute an encryption
of the addition of the bits,

00:53:06.560 --> 00:53:09.110 align:middle line:84%
an encryption of the
multiplication of the bits,

00:53:09.110 --> 00:53:10.490 align:middle line:90%
and so on and so forth.

00:53:10.490 --> 00:53:12.680 align:middle line:90%
It can do arbitrary computation.

00:53:12.680 --> 00:53:15.040 align:middle line:84%
It can compute an
arbitrary circuit c.

00:53:15.040 --> 00:53:21.130 align:middle line:90%
c goes from 01 to the n to 01.

00:53:21.130 --> 00:53:26.290 align:middle line:84%
It can do arbitrary
computation under the FHE.

00:53:26.290 --> 00:53:28.180 align:middle line:90%
So I'll just write--

00:53:28.180 --> 00:53:30.880 align:middle line:84%
maybe I'll write here
the completeness property

00:53:30.880 --> 00:53:31.850 align:middle line:90%
for the eval.

00:53:31.850 --> 00:53:34.720 align:middle line:84%
So I wrote the completeness
property where all you do

00:53:34.720 --> 00:53:36.100 align:middle line:90%
is decrypt.

00:53:36.100 --> 00:53:38.740 align:middle line:84%
If you encrypt and decrypt,
you get back the message.

00:53:38.740 --> 00:53:41.560 align:middle line:84%
The eval also has a
completeness property

00:53:41.560 --> 00:53:44.700 align:middle line:84%
which essentially says-- so
let me just write it down.

00:53:44.700 --> 00:53:46.845 align:middle line:84%
So completeness of eval,
I'll write it here.

00:53:46.845 --> 00:53:54.790 align:middle line:90%


00:53:54.790 --> 00:54:04.400 align:middle line:84%
It says that for every b1
up to bn and the probability

00:54:04.400 --> 00:54:08.130 align:middle line:90%
that when you decrypt eval--

00:54:08.130 --> 00:54:19.980 align:middle line:84%
sorry, for every c, for every
circuit c, when you decrypt--

00:54:19.980 --> 00:54:22.250 align:middle line:84%
so choose a public
key, secret key pair.

00:54:22.250 --> 00:54:28.790 align:middle line:90%


00:54:28.790 --> 00:54:31.580 align:middle line:84%
When you decrypt,
use the secret key.

00:54:31.580 --> 00:54:34.670 align:middle line:84%
To decrypt the
eval function, so,

00:54:34.670 --> 00:54:40.280 align:middle line:84%
now, evaluate with respect to
the public key and the circuit

00:54:40.280 --> 00:54:40.970 align:middle line:90%
c.

00:54:40.970 --> 00:54:46.490 align:middle line:84%
Evaluate on this--
and ciphertext

00:54:46.490 --> 00:54:48.290 align:middle line:90%
corresponding to b1 up to bn.

00:54:48.290 --> 00:54:54.530 align:middle line:84%
So encryption public key
with b1 up to encryption

00:54:54.530 --> 00:55:02.220 align:middle line:84%
of public key and bit bn, so
encrypt each of these bits.

00:55:02.220 --> 00:55:04.550 align:middle line:84%
You'll get encryption of
b1 up to encryption bn.

00:55:04.550 --> 00:55:06.050 align:middle line:90%
Then evaluate.

00:55:06.050 --> 00:55:09.290 align:middle line:84%
Do homomorphic computations
on these encrypted bits

00:55:09.290 --> 00:55:10.540 align:middle line:90%
with respect to circuit c.

00:55:10.540 --> 00:55:13.480 align:middle line:90%


00:55:13.480 --> 00:55:14.960 align:middle line:90%
Now, you get a ciphertext.

00:55:14.960 --> 00:55:19.480 align:middle line:84%
You should get a ciphertext
that encrypts c of b1 up to bn.

00:55:19.480 --> 00:55:25.090 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, the probability that
when you decrypt you get c of b1

00:55:25.090 --> 00:55:29.280 align:middle line:90%
up to bn is 1 or close to 1.

00:55:29.280 --> 00:55:37.210 align:middle line:90%


00:55:37.210 --> 00:55:41.380 align:middle line:84%
So again, what eval does,
it does a computation

00:55:41.380 --> 00:55:43.030 align:middle line:90%
on this encrypted data.

00:55:43.030 --> 00:55:47.530 align:middle line:84%
It computes an
arbitrary circuit c

00:55:47.530 --> 00:55:49.360 align:middle line:84%
that, when you
decrypt it-- and did

00:55:49.360 --> 00:55:52.180 align:middle line:84%
you get c with probability
very, very close to 1.

00:55:52.180 --> 00:55:54.550 align:middle line:84%
I wrote here 1 minus
negligible as opposed

00:55:54.550 --> 00:55:58.300 align:middle line:84%
to 1 because our scheme have
this negative probability

00:55:58.300 --> 00:55:58.970 align:middle line:90%
of error.

00:55:58.970 --> 00:56:00.820 align:middle line:90%
So that's why I--

00:56:00.820 --> 00:56:04.910 align:middle line:90%


00:56:04.910 --> 00:56:09.470 align:middle line:84%
So again, high level what
the FHE, Fully Homomorphic

00:56:09.470 --> 00:56:12.440 align:middle line:84%
Encryption, allows you to
do is to do this computation

00:56:12.440 --> 00:56:13.345 align:middle line:90%
on encrypted data.

00:56:13.345 --> 00:56:17.730 align:middle line:90%


00:56:17.730 --> 00:56:20.340 align:middle line:84%
Questions about the fully
homomorphic encryption?

00:56:20.340 --> 00:56:24.370 align:middle line:90%


00:56:24.370 --> 00:56:25.300 align:middle line:90%
We're good?

00:56:25.300 --> 00:56:26.080 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:56:26.080 --> 00:56:29.540 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess does
it matter what c is?

00:56:29.540 --> 00:56:30.490 align:middle line:90%
Oh, never mind.

00:56:30.490 --> 00:56:31.060 align:middle line:90%
Never mind.

00:56:31.060 --> 00:56:33.377 align:middle line:84%
I was thinking just
arithmetic or--

00:56:33.377 --> 00:56:34.460 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Good, good.

00:56:34.460 --> 00:56:36.257 align:middle line:84%
No, that's actually
a very good question.

00:56:36.257 --> 00:56:37.340 align:middle line:90%
It's a very good question.

00:56:37.340 --> 00:56:40.250 align:middle line:84%
So the question was this
circuit c, what is it?

00:56:40.250 --> 00:56:43.030 align:middle line:84%
So the circuit c, think
of it as a circuit that

00:56:43.030 --> 00:56:44.740 align:middle line:84%
has addition and
multiplication gates

00:56:44.740 --> 00:56:51.440 align:middle line:84%
mod 2, so any polynomial size
circuit that has addition

00:56:51.440 --> 00:56:53.180 align:middle line:90%
and multiplication gates mod 2.

00:56:53.180 --> 00:56:55.600 align:middle line:90%
Great, thank you.

00:56:55.600 --> 00:56:56.720 align:middle line:90%
Other questions?

00:56:56.720 --> 00:57:00.310 align:middle line:90%


00:57:00.310 --> 00:57:01.290 align:middle line:90%
So now, let's go back.

00:57:01.290 --> 00:57:02.790 align:middle line:84%
So now, we have an
encryption scheme

00:57:02.790 --> 00:57:05.440 align:middle line:84%
that we can do computation
and encrypted data.

00:57:05.440 --> 00:57:06.850 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's go back.

00:57:06.850 --> 00:57:08.470 align:middle line:90%
Remember, where are we?

00:57:08.470 --> 00:57:11.580 align:middle line:84%
We're saying all we want
is to avoid this bad.

00:57:11.580 --> 00:57:13.530 align:middle line:84%
We want a hash function,
its only purpose

00:57:13.530 --> 00:57:15.790 align:middle line:90%
to avoid one single point.

00:57:15.790 --> 00:57:19.190 align:middle line:90%
How hard can that be?

00:57:19.190 --> 00:57:20.900 align:middle line:84%
I'll tell you the
hard problem is

00:57:20.900 --> 00:57:23.610 align:middle line:84%
it needs to avoid the bad
point for each and every alpha.

00:57:23.610 --> 00:57:26.160 align:middle line:84%
That's kind of the hard
part that, for every alpha,

00:57:26.160 --> 00:57:27.580 align:middle line:90%
you need to avoid the bad one.

00:57:27.580 --> 00:57:29.820 align:middle line:84%
If you just want to avoid
the bad one for one alpha,

00:57:29.820 --> 00:57:30.483 align:middle line:90%
choose random.

00:57:30.483 --> 00:57:32.400 align:middle line:84%
Probably, you're not
going to hit the bad one.

00:57:32.400 --> 00:57:36.450 align:middle line:84%
But you need to avoid the bad
one for each and every alpha.

00:57:36.450 --> 00:57:40.260 align:middle line:90%
That's kind of harder.

00:57:40.260 --> 00:57:43.003 align:middle line:84%
But if we have the
trapdoor, then it's easy.

00:57:43.003 --> 00:57:44.920 align:middle line:84%
But we said, look, we
can't give the trapdoor.

00:57:44.920 --> 00:57:47.780 align:middle line:90%
That will break zero knowledge.

00:57:47.780 --> 00:57:49.230 align:middle line:84%
So instead, we
say, you know what?

00:57:49.230 --> 00:57:53.020 align:middle line:84%
Let's give a homomorphic
encryption of the trapdoor.

00:57:53.020 --> 00:57:56.110 align:middle line:90%


00:57:56.110 --> 00:57:58.510 align:middle line:90%
Now, this seems not helpful.

00:57:58.510 --> 00:58:00.220 align:middle line:90%
Why is it not helpful?

00:58:00.220 --> 00:58:04.915 align:middle line:84%
OK, let's say you can
compute on encrypted data.

00:58:04.915 --> 00:58:08.700 align:middle line:84%
You do the computation
of computing bad.

00:58:08.700 --> 00:58:10.100 align:middle line:90%
So now, here's my hash function.

00:58:10.100 --> 00:58:12.400 align:middle line:90%
It has trapdoor, hardwire.

00:58:12.400 --> 00:58:15.940 align:middle line:84%
So think of it like h of,
instead of trapdoor, it

00:58:15.940 --> 00:58:17.720 align:middle line:90%
will have hat of trapdoor.

00:58:17.720 --> 00:58:19.240 align:middle line:90%
Why not?

00:58:19.240 --> 00:58:23.590 align:middle line:84%
And it will do this computation
under the hood with eval

00:58:23.590 --> 00:58:26.390 align:middle line:84%
under the hood of the
encryption, very nice.

00:58:26.390 --> 00:58:35.070 align:middle line:84%
So now, on input alpha,
you won't get the bad alpha

00:58:35.070 --> 00:58:38.280 align:middle line:84%
plus 1, very nice,
but encrypted.

00:58:38.280 --> 00:58:40.910 align:middle line:90%
So why is that helpful?

00:58:40.910 --> 00:58:41.770 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:58:41.770 --> 00:58:46.145 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] you could
also encrypt the eval part, so

00:58:46.145 --> 00:58:49.130 align:middle line:84%
that encryption, the ciphertext,
the two different messages

00:58:49.130 --> 00:58:50.040 align:middle line:90%
are now equal.

00:58:50.040 --> 00:58:51.790 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: Again,
what are you saying?

00:58:51.790 --> 00:58:52.460 align:middle line:90%
Encrypt?

00:58:52.460 --> 00:58:54.260 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Encrypt the eval--

00:58:54.260 --> 00:58:55.790 align:middle line:84%
the left-hand side
of the equality,

00:58:55.790 --> 00:58:57.920 align:middle line:90%
the eval hash [INAUDIBLE].

00:58:57.920 --> 00:58:59.580 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: Oh,
you can encrypt--

00:58:59.580 --> 00:59:00.920 align:middle line:90%
exactly.

00:59:00.920 --> 00:59:05.110 align:middle line:84%
So that's kind of
what I'm doing.

00:59:05.110 --> 00:59:08.380 align:middle line:90%


00:59:08.380 --> 00:59:10.170 align:middle line:90%
This is the shorthand for--

00:59:10.170 --> 00:59:15.330 align:middle line:84%
this is actually eval of--
this is kind of the hash key.

00:59:15.330 --> 00:59:19.230 align:middle line:84%
The hash key will
have trapdoor in it.

00:59:19.230 --> 00:59:22.590 align:middle line:84%
And an input alpha,
it should output this.

00:59:22.590 --> 00:59:25.560 align:middle line:84%
And what we're doing is
we're saying, OK encrypt--

00:59:25.560 --> 00:59:29.070 align:middle line:84%
or you can think of it the
hash key being trapdoor

00:59:29.070 --> 00:59:31.510 align:middle line:90%
and encrypt the entire thing.

00:59:31.510 --> 00:59:35.560 align:middle line:84%
You can do everything under
the hood of the encryption.

00:59:35.560 --> 00:59:38.665 align:middle line:84%
You want the output to
be different than beta.

00:59:38.665 --> 00:59:40.660 align:middle line:90%
You get a ciphertext.

00:59:40.660 --> 00:59:44.780 align:middle line:84%
I want this to be
different than beta.

00:59:44.780 --> 00:59:46.690 align:middle line:90%
That's what I want.

00:59:46.690 --> 00:59:49.000 align:middle line:84%
I don't want underneath
the hood to be something

00:59:49.000 --> 00:59:51.410 align:middle line:90%
that's different than beta.

00:59:51.410 --> 00:59:54.470 align:middle line:90%
So this seems like a bad idea.

00:59:54.470 --> 00:59:57.620 align:middle line:90%
It gets us nowhere.

00:59:57.620 --> 00:59:59.130 align:middle line:90%
Yes, again, where are we?

00:59:59.130 --> 01:00:02.718 align:middle line:84%
We said, if we knew
trapdoor, we would be done.

01:00:02.718 --> 01:00:04.760 align:middle line:84%
The hash function, the
eval of the hash function,

01:00:04.760 --> 01:00:08.420 align:middle line:84%
will take this trapdoor, compute
beta, and output not beta,

01:00:08.420 --> 01:00:10.710 align:middle line:84%
move it by one, move one of
the bits by one, whatever,

01:00:10.710 --> 01:00:13.360 align:middle line:84%
as long as it doesn't--
it's not the beta.

01:00:13.360 --> 01:00:16.360 align:middle line:84%
And now, they say, no, let's
do everything under the hood.

01:00:16.360 --> 01:00:17.160 align:middle line:90%
What?

01:00:17.160 --> 01:00:18.530 align:middle line:90%
Then we'll get an encryption.

01:00:18.530 --> 01:00:19.960 align:middle line:90%
Why would that not be--

01:00:19.960 --> 01:00:25.153 align:middle line:84%
it seems like you're not
getting anywhere to me.

01:00:25.153 --> 01:00:27.320 align:middle line:84%
It seemed like, when they
first presented this idea,

01:00:27.320 --> 01:00:29.180 align:middle line:84%
I'm like, OK, this seems
completely useless.

01:00:29.180 --> 01:00:29.680 align:middle line:90%
So what?

01:00:29.680 --> 01:00:31.770 align:middle line:84%
Now, everything
is under the hood.

01:00:31.770 --> 01:00:32.560 align:middle line:90%
And guess what?

01:00:32.560 --> 01:00:33.310 align:middle line:90%
We're almost done.

01:00:33.310 --> 01:00:34.070 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:00:34.070 --> 01:00:36.360 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:00:36.360 --> 01:00:39.580 align:middle line:90%


01:00:39.580 --> 01:00:43.050 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: You
take the randomness.

01:00:43.050 --> 01:00:45.780 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Define the randomness
so that computes encryption

01:00:45.780 --> 01:00:49.205 align:middle line:90%
of eval plus 1 gets us that--

01:00:49.205 --> 01:00:51.937 align:middle line:90%


01:00:51.937 --> 01:00:53.270 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: So you know what?

01:00:53.270 --> 01:00:55.030 align:middle line:84%
I want to say something
even before that.

01:00:55.030 --> 01:00:57.300 align:middle line:90%
There's even a mismatch.

01:00:57.300 --> 01:01:00.562 align:middle line:84%
Beta, let's say,
consists of lambda bits.

01:01:00.562 --> 01:01:02.520 align:middle line:84%
For example, in our case,
because we repeat it,

01:01:02.520 --> 01:01:03.870 align:middle line:90%
its lambda bits.

01:01:03.870 --> 01:01:05.947 align:middle line:90%
This is not even lambda bits.

01:01:05.947 --> 01:01:08.530 align:middle line:84%
It's much more than lambda bits
because we encrypt bit by bit.

01:01:08.530 --> 01:01:09.340 align:middle line:90%
It's much bigger.

01:01:09.340 --> 01:01:12.190 align:middle line:90%


01:01:12.190 --> 01:01:14.340 align:middle line:90%
It seems like completely bogus.

01:01:14.340 --> 01:01:18.030 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be the
length of beta times security

01:01:18.030 --> 01:01:18.840 align:middle line:90%
parameter.

01:01:18.840 --> 01:01:23.340 align:middle line:84%
So it's not it doesn't
even correspond to--

01:01:23.340 --> 01:01:27.410 align:middle line:90%
the sizes don't even match.

01:01:27.410 --> 01:01:31.970 align:middle line:84%
So it seems like an
encryption is not helpful.

01:01:31.970 --> 01:01:32.470 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:01:32.470 --> 01:01:35.210 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How could
it computer beta

01:01:35.210 --> 01:01:37.546 align:middle line:90%
bad given alpha has trapdoor?

01:01:37.546 --> 01:01:41.057 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: Oh, Good The
only way he can compute beta bad

01:01:41.057 --> 01:01:43.640 align:middle line:84%
is if he has the-- oh, how can
you compute given the trapdoor,

01:01:43.640 --> 01:01:44.225 align:middle line:90%
you're saying?

01:01:44.225 --> 01:01:44.630 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:01:44.630 --> 01:01:45.380 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: OK.

01:01:45.380 --> 01:01:47.060 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Then it
will [INAUDIBLE]?

01:01:47.060 --> 01:01:47.360 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Yeah.

01:01:47.360 --> 01:01:48.390 align:middle line:90%
So what he will do?

01:01:48.390 --> 01:01:51.050 align:middle line:84%
The trapdoor in
this protocol allows

01:01:51.050 --> 01:01:54.320 align:middle line:90%
you to open the commitment.

01:01:54.320 --> 01:01:58.990 align:middle line:84%
So now, you see each point
if it has a 01, 01, 01.

01:01:58.990 --> 01:02:01.690 align:middle line:84%
So now, why do you
know what's beta bad?

01:02:01.690 --> 01:02:04.150 align:middle line:90%
Because, now, you look.

01:02:04.150 --> 01:02:08.488 align:middle line:84%
If it has just a
Hamiltonian cycle,

01:02:08.488 --> 01:02:10.280 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the truth is,
it's easier to see it

01:02:10.280 --> 01:02:12.200 align:middle line:90%
if you have the pi.

01:02:12.200 --> 01:02:13.900 align:middle line:84%
Suppose you always
also committed to pi.

01:02:13.900 --> 01:02:15.370 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's just easier
to see it that way.

01:02:15.370 --> 01:02:17.400 align:middle line:84%
Suppose I tell you, don't
only commit to that.

01:02:17.400 --> 01:02:19.850 align:middle line:90%
Commit also to your pi.

01:02:19.850 --> 01:02:22.070 align:middle line:90%
Now, you see what's in there.

01:02:22.070 --> 01:02:23.560 align:middle line:90%
Either if you remove pi--

01:02:23.560 --> 01:02:30.790 align:middle line:90%


01:02:30.790 --> 01:02:34.030 align:middle line:84%
so you look at all
the edges of pi of g.

01:02:34.030 --> 01:02:38.020 align:middle line:84%
And you make sure that
these edges are 0.

01:02:38.020 --> 01:02:39.080 align:middle line:90%
Everywhere, they're 0.

01:02:39.080 --> 01:02:43.460 align:middle line:84%
If one of them is 1, you
know that he won't succeed.

01:02:43.460 --> 01:02:45.120 align:middle line:90%
Then this is not bad.

01:02:45.120 --> 01:02:49.440 align:middle line:84%
Then the b equals 1 is not
bad because he will fail.

01:02:49.440 --> 01:02:52.730 align:middle line:84%
The bad is where
he will succeed.

01:02:52.730 --> 01:02:55.880 align:middle line:84%
A bad beta is the beta that
will allow him to succeed.

01:02:55.880 --> 01:02:59.360 align:middle line:84%
So now, if you can open
everything, now you can see.

01:02:59.360 --> 01:03:03.170 align:middle line:84%
If there is one edge
of pi of g, if there

01:03:03.170 --> 01:03:06.175 align:middle line:84%
is a nonedge that
has a 1, then you

01:03:06.175 --> 01:03:08.820 align:middle line:90%
know this is definitely not bad.

01:03:08.820 --> 01:03:12.090 align:middle line:84%
And so he won't be able to see--
so, this, this is the beta bad.

01:03:12.090 --> 01:03:16.640 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, if all of
the nonedges are, indeed, 0,

01:03:16.640 --> 01:03:19.400 align:middle line:84%
you know he can't open
Hamiltonian cycle because there

01:03:19.400 --> 01:03:22.970 align:middle line:90%
is no Hamiltonian cycle.

01:03:22.970 --> 01:03:24.350 align:middle line:90%
So then this becomes the bad.

01:03:24.350 --> 01:03:26.400 align:middle line:84%
So if you know what's
in the commitment,

01:03:26.400 --> 01:03:27.990 align:middle line:84%
you can tell which
one is the bad.

01:03:27.990 --> 01:03:30.720 align:middle line:84%
So the trapdoor allows you to
compute the bad efficiently.

01:03:30.720 --> 01:03:32.070 align:middle line:90%
That's the point.

01:03:32.070 --> 01:03:32.620 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:03:32.620 --> 01:03:35.120 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So does that mean the
[INAUDIBLE] the random coins

01:03:35.120 --> 01:03:37.640 align:middle line:90%
used in each [INAUDIBLE]?

01:03:37.640 --> 01:03:39.410 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: The
random coins used?

01:03:39.410 --> 01:03:42.080 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The randomness
that's used inside each--

01:03:42.080 --> 01:03:43.355 align:middle line:90%
is that what the trapdoor is?

01:03:43.355 --> 01:03:45.870 align:middle line:90%


01:03:45.870 --> 01:03:47.928 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: No.

01:03:47.928 --> 01:03:49.470 align:middle line:84%
I'm thinking of the
trapdoor as being

01:03:49.470 --> 01:03:52.407 align:middle line:84%
the secret key corresponding
to the public key.

01:03:52.407 --> 01:03:53.490 align:middle line:90%
I'll tell you the problem.

01:03:53.490 --> 01:03:55.740 align:middle line:84%
The problem of using
the randomness,

01:03:55.740 --> 01:03:58.890 align:middle line:84%
then it's chosen by
the cheating prover.

01:03:58.890 --> 01:04:01.440 align:middle line:84%
I don't know how
he's going to use it.

01:04:01.440 --> 01:04:05.005 align:middle line:84%
The trapdoor, you
write the trapdoor--

01:04:05.005 --> 01:04:06.130 align:middle line:90%
this is an important point.

01:04:06.130 --> 01:04:10.830 align:middle line:84%
So note, I can open
this commitment

01:04:10.830 --> 01:04:13.860 align:middle line:84%
by giving you the
randomness that I used

01:04:13.860 --> 01:04:15.460 align:middle line:90%
to generate this commitment.

01:04:15.460 --> 01:04:17.180 align:middle line:90%
That would be a good trapdoor.

01:04:17.180 --> 01:04:20.310 align:middle line:84%
But when I designed the
Fiat-Shamir hash function,

01:04:20.310 --> 01:04:22.442 align:middle line:84%
I have no idea what
randomness the cheating

01:04:22.442 --> 01:04:23.400 align:middle line:90%
prover is going to use.

01:04:23.400 --> 01:04:24.450 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it's even deterministic.

01:04:24.450 --> 01:04:25.658 align:middle line:90%
I don't know what he's doing.

01:04:25.658 --> 01:04:27.110 align:middle line:90%
He may just give me--

01:04:27.110 --> 01:04:28.110 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

01:04:28.110 --> 01:04:31.382 align:middle line:84%
So I don't know ahead of
time it's random coins.

01:04:31.382 --> 01:04:32.840 align:middle line:84%
And different
cheating provers will

01:04:32.840 --> 01:04:34.670 align:middle line:90%
have different random coins.

01:04:34.670 --> 01:04:36.475 align:middle line:84%
So I want to put
something in the CRS,

01:04:36.475 --> 01:04:39.210 align:middle line:84%
in the hash function, the
Fiat-Shamir hash function.

01:04:39.210 --> 01:04:40.670 align:middle line:84%
So this trapdoor
is actually going

01:04:40.670 --> 01:04:44.760 align:middle line:84%
to be the secret key
corresponding to the public key.

01:04:44.760 --> 01:04:47.350 align:middle line:84%
The secret key is not
going to be used in here.

01:04:47.350 --> 01:04:49.380 align:middle line:84%
It's never going to be
used in the protocol,

01:04:49.380 --> 01:04:51.730 align:middle line:84%
but it will be used
in the analysis.

01:04:51.730 --> 01:04:54.320 align:middle line:90%


01:04:54.320 --> 01:04:55.080 align:middle line:90%
OK?

01:04:55.080 --> 01:04:55.620 align:middle line:90%
Great.

01:04:55.620 --> 01:04:56.220 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:04:56.220 --> 01:05:00.890 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is this only if you
do an interactive proof where

01:05:00.890 --> 01:05:03.530 align:middle line:84%
you commit to the
witness, or can you

01:05:03.530 --> 01:05:07.100 align:middle line:84%
draw this to put
first [INAUDIBLE]?

01:05:07.100 --> 01:05:09.240 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: Again,
you're saying, is this--

01:05:09.240 --> 01:05:12.290 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: To proof system where
you commit to the witness,

01:05:12.290 --> 01:05:15.920 align:middle line:84%
or can you draw
this to proof system

01:05:15.920 --> 01:05:18.302 align:middle line:90%
where the first [INAUDIBLE]?

01:05:18.302 --> 01:05:24.707 align:middle line:90%


01:05:24.707 --> 01:05:26.290 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: So
this technique works

01:05:26.290 --> 01:05:30.440 align:middle line:84%
for this protocol where you
commit to something in open.

01:05:30.440 --> 01:05:32.843 align:middle line:90%
And after, I'll show you why.

01:05:32.843 --> 01:05:34.760 align:middle line:84%
I'll show you that it's
actually more general.

01:05:34.760 --> 01:05:38.300 align:middle line:84%
And it applies to a large,
actually, slew of protocols

01:05:38.300 --> 01:05:40.110 align:middle line:84%
where there's no
commitment whatsoever.

01:05:40.110 --> 01:05:42.620 align:middle line:84%
For example, it
applies to GCR protocol

01:05:42.620 --> 01:05:44.070 align:middle line:90%
where it's just sum-check.

01:05:44.070 --> 01:05:46.010 align:middle line:84%
It applies to
sum-check protocol.

01:05:46.010 --> 01:05:49.810 align:middle line:84%
And so it's not
specific to this.

01:05:49.810 --> 01:05:52.150 align:middle line:84%
And I'll show you
later all you need

01:05:52.150 --> 01:05:56.080 align:middle line:84%
is this trapdoor that tells you
where the bad kind of challenges

01:05:56.080 --> 01:05:56.640 align:middle line:90%
are.

01:05:56.640 --> 01:05:58.432 align:middle line:84%
And once you have a
trapdoor that tells you

01:05:58.432 --> 01:06:02.510 align:middle line:84%
where the bad challenges
are, then you can use it.

01:06:02.510 --> 01:06:05.630 align:middle line:84%
But we'll mention that
at the end of class,

01:06:05.630 --> 01:06:06.610 align:middle line:90%
like towards the end.

01:06:06.610 --> 01:06:09.010 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, we'll see
that it's general.

01:06:09.010 --> 01:06:12.190 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, you don't even
need to look too, too detailed

01:06:12.190 --> 01:06:13.750 align:middle line:90%
into that protocol.

01:06:13.750 --> 01:06:15.380 align:middle line:84%
The idea here is
more high level.

01:06:15.380 --> 01:06:18.070 align:middle line:84%
We say, look, in an
interactive proof,

01:06:18.070 --> 01:06:23.350 align:middle line:84%
there's the beta that are
bad, bad for security, that

01:06:23.350 --> 01:06:24.740 align:middle line:90%
are good for the adversary.

01:06:24.740 --> 01:06:26.180 align:middle line:90%
But they're bad for security.

01:06:26.180 --> 01:06:29.020 align:middle line:84%
If the adversary managed
to hit a bad beta

01:06:29.020 --> 01:06:33.500 align:middle line:84%
and alpha for which h of
alpha gives him a bad beta,

01:06:33.500 --> 01:06:34.760 align:middle line:90%
then he can cheat me.

01:06:34.760 --> 01:06:36.820 align:middle line:90%
I want to avoid these bad betas.

01:06:36.820 --> 01:06:38.320 align:middle line:84%
And now, we're
saying, well, there's

01:06:38.320 --> 01:06:40.220 align:middle line:84%
only one bad beta
for simplicity.

01:06:40.220 --> 01:06:42.710 align:middle line:84%
This one has one bad
beta, so we're good.

01:06:42.710 --> 01:06:46.240 align:middle line:84%
If it has one bad beta,
I want to avoid that one.

01:06:46.240 --> 01:06:49.270 align:middle line:84%
Now, if there's
some trapdoor that

01:06:49.270 --> 01:06:53.350 align:middle line:84%
allows me to compute
that bad beta,

01:06:53.350 --> 01:06:57.190 align:middle line:84%
then I can't put the
trapdoor in the hash function

01:06:57.190 --> 01:06:58.810 align:middle line:90%
and give it to the world.

01:06:58.810 --> 01:07:01.490 align:middle line:84%
There's a reason why it's
a trapdoor and not public.

01:07:01.490 --> 01:07:04.030 align:middle line:84%
Here, in this case,
it's for zero knowledge.

01:07:04.030 --> 01:07:09.220 align:middle line:84%
But what I can do is give an
encryption of the trapdoor.

01:07:09.220 --> 01:07:10.760 align:middle line:84%
And actually, in
the actual scheme,

01:07:10.760 --> 01:07:11.950 align:middle line:84%
it won't be encryption
on that trapdoor.

01:07:11.950 --> 01:07:13.117 align:middle line:90%
It'll be encryption of zero.

01:07:13.117 --> 01:07:15.160 align:middle line:84%
Nobody's going to ever
decrypt this thing.

01:07:15.160 --> 01:07:16.960 align:middle line:84%
But let's think of
it as an encryption

01:07:16.960 --> 01:07:20.210 align:middle line:90%
of the trapdoor for now.

01:07:20.210 --> 01:07:22.110 align:middle line:90%
Now, again, where are we?

01:07:22.110 --> 01:07:24.070 align:middle line:84%
Why did we make
any progress here?

01:07:24.070 --> 01:07:29.510 align:middle line:90%


01:07:29.510 --> 01:07:35.330 align:middle line:84%
So next, is the most
amazing point, I think.

01:07:35.330 --> 01:07:37.790 align:middle line:84%
When I saw this, it
took me a very long time

01:07:37.790 --> 01:07:40.705 align:middle line:84%
to process because it's
such an ingenious idea.

01:07:40.705 --> 01:07:43.310 align:middle line:90%


01:07:43.310 --> 01:07:45.960 align:middle line:90%
But here's the idea.

01:07:45.960 --> 01:07:49.850 align:middle line:84%
So they say, yeah,
you know what?

01:07:49.850 --> 01:07:54.920 align:middle line:90%
Nevertheless, my hash function--

01:07:54.920 --> 01:07:57.060 align:middle line:84%
so then they say,
OK, you're right.

01:07:57.060 --> 01:08:02.390 align:middle line:84%
This kind of hash function
that has the trapdoor in it

01:08:02.390 --> 01:08:04.877 align:middle line:84%
and you evaluate the
trapdoor, you're right.

01:08:04.877 --> 01:08:05.460 align:middle line:90%
It's not good.

01:08:05.460 --> 01:08:06.800 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't even type check.

01:08:06.800 --> 01:08:09.150 align:middle line:84%
The output is not the
length it's supposed to be.

01:08:09.150 --> 01:08:10.550 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, this is nonsense.

01:08:10.550 --> 01:08:12.140 align:middle line:90%
Let us fix this.

01:08:12.140 --> 01:08:13.950 align:middle line:90%
And here's how they fix it.

01:08:13.950 --> 01:08:17.240 align:middle line:84%
Let me put the eval here
because it'd be nice to see.

01:08:17.240 --> 01:08:19.118 align:middle line:90%
So here's their fix.

01:08:19.118 --> 01:08:20.160 align:middle line:90%
They said, you know what?

01:08:20.160 --> 01:08:23.149 align:middle line:84%
Fine, that may have
been the wrong function.

01:08:23.149 --> 01:08:24.600 align:middle line:90%
We're going to encrypt.

01:08:24.600 --> 01:08:29.960 align:middle line:84%
So our CRR hash key is going
to consist of encryption

01:08:29.960 --> 01:08:33.080 align:middle line:90%
of another function, g.

01:08:33.080 --> 01:08:35.950 align:middle line:84%
By the way, I'm using
the hat to denote

01:08:35.950 --> 01:08:38.950 align:middle line:84%
fully homomorphic encryption as
opposed to standard encryption.

01:08:38.950 --> 01:08:41.109 align:middle line:84%
That's often we use
in the literature.

01:08:41.109 --> 01:08:44.620 align:middle line:84%
The hat is kind of a shortcut
to denote public key comma

01:08:44.620 --> 01:08:45.880 align:middle line:90%
encryption of g.

01:08:45.880 --> 01:08:48.580 align:middle line:84%
And when you use
this hat, we usually

01:08:48.580 --> 01:08:50.260 align:middle line:84%
use it when we want
to talk about fully

01:08:50.260 --> 01:08:53.149 align:middle line:90%
homomorphic encryption.

01:08:53.149 --> 01:08:57.010 align:middle line:84%
So now, they say, this
is going to be my--

01:08:57.010 --> 01:08:58.779 align:middle line:84%
nevertheless, I'm
going to encrypt a g.

01:08:58.779 --> 01:08:59.899 align:middle line:90%
What's this g?

01:08:59.899 --> 01:09:01.060 align:middle line:90%
We'll see.

01:09:01.060 --> 01:09:02.290 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to encrypt a g.

01:09:02.290 --> 01:09:06.069 align:middle line:90%


01:09:06.069 --> 01:09:08.560 align:middle line:90%
So here's their hash key.

01:09:08.560 --> 01:09:13.720 align:middle line:84%
The hash key is going to be I'm
going to generate a public key

01:09:13.720 --> 01:09:15.870 align:middle line:90%
and encrypt a function g.

01:09:15.870 --> 01:09:18.420 align:middle line:84%
So it's like the public
key corresponding to g

01:09:18.420 --> 01:09:21.270 align:middle line:84%
and then g, which is
just another shortcut

01:09:21.270 --> 01:09:23.939 align:middle line:90%
to say encryption of g.

01:09:23.939 --> 01:09:28.560 align:middle line:84%
And when I say encrypted g,
just think of the description

01:09:28.560 --> 01:09:29.865 align:middle line:90%
of the circuit.

01:09:29.865 --> 01:09:31.740 align:middle line:84%
So there is some way to
describe the circuit.

01:09:31.740 --> 01:09:34.700 align:middle line:90%
I encrypt the description.

01:09:34.700 --> 01:09:36.970 align:middle line:84%
So I encrypt this
description of a circuit g.

01:09:36.970 --> 01:09:40.310 align:middle line:90%
What this g is we'll see.

01:09:40.310 --> 01:09:41.890 align:middle line:90%
But that's my hash key.

01:09:41.890 --> 01:09:45.640 align:middle line:90%
So this is the hash key.

01:09:45.640 --> 01:09:51.850 align:middle line:84%
How do you evaluate
hash key and alpha?

01:09:51.850 --> 01:09:52.939 align:middle line:90%
They say you know what?

01:09:52.939 --> 01:09:56.920 align:middle line:84%
Just compute g of
alpha under the hood.

01:09:56.920 --> 01:09:58.830 align:middle line:84%
How do you compute g of
alpha under the hood.

01:09:58.830 --> 01:10:03.520 align:middle line:84%
Essentially, they say, do
they eval of the f and g.

01:10:03.520 --> 01:10:05.710 align:middle line:84%
There's two evals, so
it's a bit confusing.

01:10:05.710 --> 01:10:09.250 align:middle line:84%
Because we usually denote
the evaluation computing

01:10:09.250 --> 01:10:10.600 align:middle line:90%
the hash function by eval.

01:10:10.600 --> 01:10:13.570 align:middle line:84%
But now, we also have the
eval of the full homomorphic

01:10:13.570 --> 01:10:14.710 align:middle line:90%
encryption scheme.

01:10:14.710 --> 01:10:17.950 align:middle line:84%
So take the fully homomorphic
encryption scheme.

01:10:17.950 --> 01:10:19.970 align:middle line:90%
Take the ciphertext g.

01:10:19.970 --> 01:10:24.070 align:middle line:84%
So take the public key
corresponding to the ciphertext.

01:10:24.070 --> 01:10:25.220 align:middle line:90%
What's the function?

01:10:25.220 --> 01:10:26.390 align:middle line:90%
What's the circuit c?

01:10:26.390 --> 01:10:29.355 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be kind of a
universal circuit sub alpha.

01:10:29.355 --> 01:10:31.795 align:middle line:90%


01:10:31.795 --> 01:10:33.420 align:middle line:84%
I'll tell you what
this is in a second.

01:10:33.420 --> 01:10:36.440 align:middle line:90%
And the ciphertext is g.

01:10:36.440 --> 01:10:39.380 align:middle line:84%
And this universal circuit
of alpha, all it does,

01:10:39.380 --> 01:10:43.040 align:middle line:84%
the definition, it takes
as input a circuit g

01:10:43.040 --> 01:10:47.000 align:middle line:90%
and it outputs g of alpha.

01:10:47.000 --> 01:10:49.490 align:middle line:84%
So essentially, what we do,
what this hash function does,

01:10:49.490 --> 01:10:50.940 align:middle line:90%
is exactly what we said before.

01:10:50.940 --> 01:10:52.850 align:middle line:90%
It takes an encryption of g.

01:10:52.850 --> 01:10:54.650 align:middle line:84%
How does it relate
to the trapdoor?

01:10:54.650 --> 01:10:55.830 align:middle line:90%
I'll tell you in a second.

01:10:55.830 --> 01:11:02.000 align:middle line:84%
Before, we said, g, it just
has the trapdoor in it.

01:11:02.000 --> 01:11:04.830 align:middle line:84%
Before, we say g is just
this g, computes bad.

01:11:04.830 --> 01:11:06.020 align:middle line:90%
And it has the trapdoor.

01:11:06.020 --> 01:11:08.310 align:middle line:90%
And we encrypt g.

01:11:08.310 --> 01:11:10.270 align:middle line:90%
Now, they say, OK, you're right.

01:11:10.270 --> 01:11:11.110 align:middle line:90%
There's a problem.

01:11:11.110 --> 01:11:12.690 align:middle line:84%
We'll tell you what
g is in a second.

01:11:12.690 --> 01:11:15.900 align:middle line:84%
But same idea, same
idea, we encrypt this g.

01:11:15.900 --> 01:11:18.090 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it'll be a little
different, but we encrypt g.

01:11:18.090 --> 01:11:22.390 align:middle line:84%
And the hash function computes
g and alpha under the hood.

01:11:22.390 --> 01:11:25.570 align:middle line:90%


01:11:25.570 --> 01:11:27.640 align:middle line:84%
So it takes the original
first message alpha

01:11:27.640 --> 01:11:31.890 align:middle line:84%
and computes the function
g under the hood.

01:11:31.890 --> 01:11:33.300 align:middle line:90%
We just went over for it.

01:11:33.300 --> 01:11:34.023 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't work.

01:11:34.023 --> 01:11:35.190 align:middle line:90%
Then you get the encryption.

01:11:35.190 --> 01:11:36.270 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't even type check.

01:11:36.270 --> 01:11:36.770 align:middle line:90%
What?

01:11:36.770 --> 01:11:40.410 align:middle line:90%


01:11:40.410 --> 01:11:42.840 align:middle line:90%
So here's their idea.

01:11:42.840 --> 01:11:46.505 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is the most beautiful
part of today's lecture.

01:11:46.505 --> 01:11:54.130 align:middle line:90%


01:11:54.130 --> 01:11:56.750 align:middle line:84%
Let me just write it
short kind of hand-way.

01:11:56.750 --> 01:12:01.660 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be g of
alpha encrypted because this

01:12:01.660 --> 01:12:04.280 align:middle line:84%
is what u does. u does,
it computes g of alpha.

01:12:04.280 --> 01:12:08.040 align:middle line:84%
So what you get back is
encryption of g of alpha.

01:12:08.040 --> 01:12:09.660 align:middle line:90%
Now, they say, OK.

01:12:09.660 --> 01:12:13.752 align:middle line:84%
And by the way, let me first
say, in their scheme, what is g?

01:12:13.752 --> 01:12:15.460 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't matter
because it's encrypted.

01:12:15.460 --> 01:12:18.200 align:middle line:84%
It's the all-zero circuit
of a certain size.

01:12:18.200 --> 01:12:20.470 align:middle line:84%
So in their scheme,
this g is going

01:12:20.470 --> 01:12:24.470 align:middle line:84%
to be all-zero
circuit of size T0.

01:12:24.470 --> 01:12:27.320 align:middle line:90%
What T is we'll see in a second.

01:12:27.320 --> 01:12:29.040 align:middle line:90%
But that's all it is.

01:12:29.040 --> 01:12:30.770 align:middle line:84%
This is the Fiat-Shamir
hash function.

01:12:30.770 --> 01:12:32.040 align:middle line:84%
The question is,
why is it secure?

01:12:32.040 --> 01:12:34.220 align:middle line:84%
Because it seems like
there is no way this works.

01:12:34.220 --> 01:12:36.980 align:middle line:84%
But let me just say,
again, the Fiat-Shamir hash

01:12:36.980 --> 01:12:39.400 align:middle line:90%
function, what does it do?

01:12:39.400 --> 01:12:40.900 align:middle line:90%
It generates a public key.

01:12:40.900 --> 01:12:45.910 align:middle line:84%
It encrypts in the actual
scheme, all-zero string,

01:12:45.910 --> 01:12:48.057 align:middle line:90%
000 up to T times.

01:12:48.057 --> 01:12:49.390 align:middle line:90%
We'll see what T is in a second.

01:12:49.390 --> 01:12:51.880 align:middle line:84%
In the analysis, we're
going to play with this g.

01:12:51.880 --> 01:12:53.740 align:middle line:90%
But this is what it is.

01:12:53.740 --> 01:12:57.550 align:middle line:84%
And when you give him
alpha, it computes

01:12:57.550 --> 01:13:00.270 align:middle line:84%
the circuit, which is
the all-zero circuit,

01:13:00.270 --> 01:13:01.010 align:middle line:90%
under the hood.

01:13:01.010 --> 01:13:02.650 align:middle line:90%
That's all it does.

01:13:02.650 --> 01:13:04.660 align:middle line:90%
And that's the b.

01:13:04.660 --> 01:13:08.010 align:middle line:90%
That's going to be the beta.

01:13:08.010 --> 01:13:10.510 align:middle line:84%
You should be very
confused, but I'll explain.

01:13:10.510 --> 01:13:11.325 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:13:11.325 --> 01:13:13.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: All-zero
circuit board?

