01:18:53.980 --> 01:18:57.290 align:middle line:90%


01:18:57.290 --> 01:19:01.560 align:middle line:84%
And I'm just going to add the
1 to it so I know you're not--

01:19:01.560 --> 01:19:03.560 align:middle line:84%
I guess we're right XOR
because we're over 01.

01:19:03.560 --> 01:19:05.280 align:middle line:90%
I'll XOR with this with 1.

01:19:05.280 --> 01:19:07.650 align:middle line:90%
That's my g in the analysis.

01:19:07.650 --> 01:19:09.750 align:middle line:84%
So in the analysis,
I'm going to say,

01:19:09.750 --> 01:19:12.390 align:middle line:84%
suppose you succeed in
breaking the Fiat-Shamir.

01:19:12.390 --> 01:19:15.620 align:middle line:84%
You're also going to
succeed with this g.

01:19:15.620 --> 01:19:19.220 align:middle line:84%
Because it's encrypted, you
don't know the difference.

01:19:19.220 --> 01:19:22.200 align:middle line:84%
You have no idea what's
underneath the encryption.

01:19:22.200 --> 01:19:24.920 align:middle line:84%
So you should also succeed
in breaking with this g.

01:19:24.920 --> 01:19:28.880 align:middle line:84%
Now, this g looks bizarre
because I'm decrypting.

01:19:28.880 --> 01:19:31.060 align:middle line:90%
This is not a ciphertext.

01:19:31.060 --> 01:19:33.455 align:middle line:84%
This is just a bunch of
strings, a 01 string.

01:19:33.455 --> 01:19:34.330 align:middle line:90%
It's a random string.

01:19:34.330 --> 01:19:35.420 align:middle line:90%
It's not ciphertext.

01:19:35.420 --> 01:19:37.280 align:middle line:84%
What does it mean to
decrypt this thing.

01:19:37.280 --> 01:19:38.865 align:middle line:90%
It's like, this makes no sense.

01:19:38.865 --> 01:19:46.130 align:middle line:90%


01:19:46.130 --> 01:19:48.520 align:middle line:84%
But if the length
of this is the--

01:19:48.520 --> 01:19:50.050 align:middle line:84%
take a ciphertext
that the length

01:19:50.050 --> 01:19:51.435 align:middle line:90%
is like these lambda bits.

01:19:51.435 --> 01:19:54.090 align:middle line:90%


01:19:54.090 --> 01:19:55.240 align:middle line:90%
Look, this is a function.

01:19:55.240 --> 01:19:57.520 align:middle line:84%
So you can compute this
function, not on a ciphertext,

01:19:57.520 --> 01:20:00.420 align:middle line:90%
but compute this function.

01:20:00.420 --> 01:20:04.230 align:middle line:84%
So I decrypt the beta message,
which is not even a ciphertext.

01:20:04.230 --> 01:20:05.460 align:middle line:90%
And I add 1.

01:20:05.460 --> 01:20:06.480 align:middle line:90%
I XORed with 1.

01:20:06.480 --> 01:20:09.305 align:middle line:84%
I XORed with anything that's not
0 just to move it from the bad.

01:20:09.305 --> 01:20:12.280 align:middle line:90%


01:20:12.280 --> 01:20:17.480 align:middle line:84%
Now, I want to argue, now, we
know that encryption of this

01:20:17.480 --> 01:20:20.200 align:middle line:90%
cannot be beta.

01:20:20.200 --> 01:20:22.070 align:middle line:90%
Why?

01:20:22.070 --> 01:20:23.330 align:middle line:90%
Now, I can argue this.

01:20:23.330 --> 01:20:24.100 align:middle line:90%
Why?

01:20:24.100 --> 01:20:30.120 align:middle line:84%
Because if it was equal,
if you decrypt this,

01:20:30.120 --> 01:20:31.740 align:middle line:84%
