poly size cheating prover.

01:22:30.430 --> 01:22:33.810 align:middle line:90%


01:22:33.810 --> 01:22:38.940 align:middle line:84%
And he fixes some
graph g that's not--

01:22:38.940 --> 01:22:48.740 align:middle line:84%
does not have a Hamiltonian
cycle and cheats and cheats

01:22:48.740 --> 01:22:51.378 align:middle line:90%
with probability big.

01:22:51.378 --> 01:22:52.670 align:middle line:90%
Let's say greater than epsilon.

01:22:52.670 --> 01:22:56.800 align:middle line:90%
Epsilon is non-negligible.

01:22:56.800 --> 01:23:02.740 align:middle line:84%
Then I'm going to say,
then I want to argue.

01:23:02.740 --> 01:23:08.250 align:middle line:84%
Then it means that
p star also succeeds

01:23:08.250 --> 01:23:15.240 align:middle line:90%
with probability close to--

01:23:15.240 --> 01:23:19.350 align:middle line:84%
epsilon maybe minus negligible,
but similar probability.

01:23:19.350 --> 01:23:21.360 align:middle line:90%
If I change the hash key--

01:23:21.360 --> 01:23:27.120 align:middle line:84%
before, the hash key was
public key and some encryption

01:23:27.120 --> 01:23:29.860 align:middle line:90%
of some bogus g.

01:23:29.860 --> 01:23:32.410 align:middle line:84%
Now, I say it will
also succeed if I take

01:23:32.410 --> 01:23:34.270 align:middle line:90%
public key and this specific g.

01:23:34.270 --> 01:23:38.230 align:middle line:90%
Let me call this g, g star.

01:23:38.230 --> 01:23:41.710 align:middle line:90%
So that's the g in the analysis.

01:23:41.710 --> 01:23:43.760 align:middle line:84%
So again, how does
the analysis go?

01:23:43.760 --> 01:23:46.720 align:middle line:84%
I say, suppose there exists
a cheating prover that

01:23:46.720 --> 01:23:49.990 align:middle line:84%
takes a false
statement and succeeds

01:23:49.990 --> 01:23:53.680 align:middle line:84%
in proving nevertheless
and convincing.

01:23:53.680 --> 01:23:56.170 align:middle line:84%
Then I want to say
that he will also

01:23:56.170 --> 01:24:12.310 align:middle line:84%
succeed with hash key which is
public key and encryption of g

01:24:12.310 --> 01:24:16.610 align:middle line:90%
star, of this g star.

01:24:16.610 --> 01:24:17.510 align:middle line:90%
Why?

01:24:17.510 --> 01:24:21.190 align:middle line:84%
By semantic security
because you can't

01:24:21.190 --> 01:24:26.240 align:middle line:84%
distinguish between encryption
of g star and encryption of g.

01:24:26.240 --> 01:24:26.750 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:24:26.750 --> 01:24:29.270 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So here, we're
using the proof is efficient?

01:24:29.270 --> 01:24:30.290 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI:
We're using the fact

01:24:30.290 --> 01:24:31.105 align:middle line:90%
that the proof is efficient.

01:24:31.105 --> 01:24:32.210 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So there's
computational soundness.

01:24:32.210 --> 01:24:33.840 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI:
Computational soundness.

01:24:33.840 --> 01:24:35.060 align:middle line:90%
Computational soundness, yes.

01:24:35.060 --> 01:24:38.810 align:middle line:84%
We're using the fact that
the poly size is efficient.

01:24:38.810 --> 01:24:40.980 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, he cannot
break semantic security.

01:24:40.980 --> 01:24:43.130 align:middle line:84%
So he cannot
distinguish between g,

01:24:43.130 --> 01:24:45.470 align:middle line:84%
whatever the bogus g that
we put in the zero's one,

01:24:45.470 --> 01:24:48.475 align:middle line:84%
the all-zero circuit or
whatever, and this g star.

01:24:48.475 --> 01:24:52.510 align:middle line:90%


01:24:52.510 --> 01:24:53.930 align:middle line:90%
I cheated slightly here.

01:24:53.930 --> 01:24:58.170 align:middle line:84%
But let me move on, and we'll
go to the cheat in a second.

01:24:58.170 --> 01:25:01.080 align:middle line:84%
But let's say you believe that
even though you shouldn't.

01:25:01.080 --> 01:25:04.760 align:middle line:84%
But it's very delicate,
so we'll go there.

01:25:04.760 --> 01:25:06.860 align:middle line:90%
OK, semantic security.

01:25:06.860 --> 01:25:10.160 align:middle line:84%
Now, I'm saying with
this g star, now,

01:25:10.160 --> 01:25:12.150 align:middle line:84%
information theoretically,
he cannot cheat.

01:25:12.150 --> 01:25:12.920 align:middle line:90%
He's stuck.

01:25:12.920 --> 01:25:14.900 align:middle line:90%
Because guess what?

01:25:14.900 --> 01:25:21.030 align:middle line:84%
You're saying he produces alpha,
beta, gamma accepting, right?

01:25:21.030 --> 01:25:23.540 align:middle line:90%
That's what he means he cheated.

01:25:23.540 --> 01:25:28.870 align:middle line:84%
It means that he got alpha
such that h of alpha is bad.

01:25:28.870 --> 01:25:30.970 align:middle line:84%
Because you can only
cheat with a bad alpha.

01:25:30.970 --> 01:25:32.980 align:middle line:84%
That's the definition
of a bad beta.

01:25:32.980 --> 01:25:34.480 align:middle line:84%
That's the definition
of a bad beta,

01:25:34.480 --> 01:25:36.220 align:middle line:90%
one that you can cheat with.

01:25:36.220 --> 01:25:40.520 align:middle line:84%
So we found an alpha so
that h of alpha is bad.

01:25:40.520 --> 01:25:44.330 align:middle line:90%
But this g star never hits bad.

01:25:44.330 --> 01:25:45.660 align:middle line:90%
That's how we made it.

01:25:45.660 --> 01:25:48.870 align:middle line:84%
It's so that g of alpha
is never the bad alpha.

01:25:48.870 --> 01:25:49.380 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:25:49.380 --> 01:25:51.842 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So g start
is [INAUDIBLE].

01:25:51.842 --> 01:25:55.010 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: Ah, that's
what I glossed over, exactly.

01:25:55.010 --> 01:25:56.150 align:middle line:90%
Good.

01:25:56.150 --> 01:25:58.050 align:middle line:90%
So that's the proof.

01:25:58.050 --> 01:26:00.200 align:middle line:90%
However, there is a cheat.

01:26:00.200 --> 01:26:05.990 align:middle line:84%
And the cheat is, look, g
star, to compute g star--

01:26:05.990 --> 01:26:08.960 align:middle line:90%
I said, just describe g star.

01:26:08.960 --> 01:26:11.060 align:middle line:84%
When I mean encrypt
g star, I mean

01:26:11.060 --> 01:26:12.830 align:middle line:84%
give the description,
like encrypt

01:26:12.830 --> 01:26:15.120 align:middle line:90%
the description of g star.

01:26:15.120 --> 01:26:19.370 align:middle line:84%
So let's recall what does
g star do. g star decrypts.

01:26:19.370 --> 01:26:24.420 align:middle line:84%
So you need to know the secret
key corresponding to public key.

01:26:24.420 --> 01:26:27.660 align:middle line:90%
We said that you can encrypt--

01:26:27.660 --> 01:26:32.310 align:middle line:84%
semantic security holds if, for
any fixed messages m0 and m1,

01:26:32.310 --> 01:26:34.133 align:middle line:90%
you cannot distinguish between--

01:26:34.133 --> 01:26:38.960 align:middle line:90%


01:26:38.960 --> 01:26:40.430 align:middle line:90%
is it here?

01:26:40.430 --> 01:26:41.120 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:26:41.120 --> 01:26:45.060 align:middle line:84%
For any fixed m0 and m1, you
cannot distinguish between

01:26:45.060 --> 01:26:47.570 align:middle line:84%
encryption of m0
encryption of m1.

01:26:47.570 --> 01:26:51.410 align:middle line:84%
But here, I'm saying you cannot
distinguish between encryption

01:26:51.410 --> 01:26:56.360 align:middle line:84%
of the secret key and encryption
of, let's say, other bits.

01:26:56.360 --> 01:26:58.550 align:middle line:90%
That's a different story.

01:26:58.550 --> 01:27:06.230 align:middle line:84%
And indeed, we assume what's
called circular secure FHE.

01:27:06.230 --> 01:27:08.978 align:middle line:90%


01:27:08.978 --> 01:27:11.270 align:middle line:84%
We assume all that you cannot
distinguish an encryption

01:27:11.270 --> 01:27:14.950 align:middle line:84%
of the secret key an
encryption of the all-zeros.

01:27:14.950 --> 01:27:16.477 align:middle line:90%
That's an assumption.

01:27:16.477 --> 01:27:17.310 align:middle line:90%
You have a question?

01:27:17.310 --> 01:27:20.000 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:27:20.000 --> 01:27:27.420 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So far it seems like
b beta, beta bad, is also given,

01:27:27.420 --> 01:27:29.530 align:middle line:90%
not computed?

01:27:29.530 --> 01:27:30.435 align:middle line:90%
How do we--

01:27:30.435 --> 01:27:31.490 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Oh, no.

01:27:31.490 --> 01:27:32.240 align:middle line:90%
No, it's computed.

01:27:32.240 --> 01:27:33.073 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, sorry, sorry.

01:27:33.073 --> 01:27:34.020 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

01:27:34.020 --> 01:27:35.120 align:middle line:90%
No, what I meant here--

01:27:35.120 --> 01:27:35.657 align:middle line:90%
OK, thanks.

01:27:35.657 --> 01:27:37.240 align:middle line:84%
Thank you very much
for your question.

01:27:37.240 --> 01:27:39.200 align:middle line:90%
Here's what g star alpha does.

01:27:39.200 --> 01:27:42.010 align:middle line:84%
g star of alpha,
it has-- actually,

01:27:42.010 --> 01:27:44.300 align:middle line:90%
let me write more carefully.

01:27:44.300 --> 01:27:49.360 align:middle line:84%
It has trapdoor and
secret key hardwire.

01:27:49.360 --> 01:27:53.980 align:middle line:84%
The first thing it does,
it computes bad alpha

01:27:53.980 --> 01:27:57.700 align:middle line:90%
using trapdoor.

01:27:57.700 --> 01:28:00.740 align:middle line:84%
This is kind corresponding
to our scheme.

01:28:00.740 --> 01:28:04.570 align:middle line:84%
Then he takes what he
got, the bad alpha, he

01:28:04.570 --> 01:28:06.380 align:middle line:90%
decrypted using the secret key.

01:28:06.380 --> 01:28:09.160 align:middle line:84%
Then he XORs 1 to the
least significant bit

01:28:09.160 --> 01:28:10.270 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

01:28:10.270 --> 01:28:12.350 align:middle line:90%
So yeah, it does compute it.

01:28:12.350 --> 01:28:15.250 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so this g star, you
need to encrypt trapdoor.

01:28:15.250 --> 01:28:17.320 align:middle line:84%
And you need to
encrypt secret key.

01:28:17.320 --> 01:28:19.370 align:middle line:84%
Now, encrypting the
trapdoor is not a problem.

01:28:19.370 --> 01:28:21.280 align:middle line:84%
It has nothing to
do with the key.

01:28:21.280 --> 01:28:23.290 align:middle line:84%
But the problem is you
also need to encrypt

01:28:23.290 --> 01:28:27.630 align:middle line:84%
the secret key corresponding
to the public key.

01:28:27.630 --> 01:28:29.250 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, thank you
for the question.

01:28:29.250 --> 01:28:30.250 align:middle line:90%
That's a great question.

01:28:30.250 --> 01:28:34.420 align:middle line:84%
So g alpha-- I want to repeat.
g star alpha, what does it do?

01:28:34.420 --> 01:28:35.380 align:middle line:90%
It takes alpha.

01:28:35.380 --> 01:28:38.910 align:middle line:84%
It first computes the
bad beta corresponding

01:28:38.910 --> 01:28:40.870 align:middle line:90%
to the alpha using the trapdoor.

01:28:40.870 --> 01:28:42.580 align:middle line:90%
Then he takes the string.

01:28:42.580 --> 01:28:45.910 align:middle line:84%
He decrypts this weird
string using the secret key.

01:28:45.910 --> 01:28:49.830 align:middle line:84%
And then he XORs the least
significant bit with 1

01:28:49.830 --> 01:28:54.061 align:middle line:84%
or any bit with 1 just
to make it not equal.

01:28:54.061 --> 01:28:55.630 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: What is trapdoor?

01:28:55.630 --> 01:28:56.755 align:middle line:90%
Is it still the secret key?

01:28:56.755 --> 01:28:58.755 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: It's the
secret key corresponding

01:28:58.755 --> 01:28:59.800 align:middle line:90%
to this commitment.

01:28:59.800 --> 01:29:01.900 align:middle line:84%
That's why I said this
trapdoor, in some sense,

01:29:01.900 --> 01:29:04.310 align:middle line:84%
is also a secret key of
a public key encryption.

01:29:04.310 --> 01:29:07.880 align:middle line:84%
But there's two
encryption going on.

01:29:07.880 --> 01:29:10.650 align:middle line:90%
One is the FHE encryption.

01:29:10.650 --> 01:29:14.580 align:middle line:84%
And one is how we
implement this commitment.

01:29:14.580 --> 01:29:17.310 align:middle line:84%
So you write that we
implement this commitment also

01:29:17.310 --> 01:29:20.093 align:middle line:84%
with an encryption scheme,
but it's better to forget it.

01:29:20.093 --> 01:29:21.510 align:middle line:84%
Because, otherwise,
it's confusing

01:29:21.510 --> 01:29:22.930 align:middle line:84%
that there's two
encryption scheme.

01:29:22.930 --> 01:29:25.180 align:middle line:84%
So here, I want to say,
suppose you have a commitment.

01:29:25.180 --> 01:29:27.390 align:middle line:84%
And there's a chapter that
allows you to open it.

01:29:27.390 --> 01:29:30.840 align:middle line:84%
Forget that this trapdoor
is also a secret key.

01:29:30.840 --> 01:29:34.108 align:middle line:84%
So now, I'm saying you need this
trapdoor to open the commitment,

01:29:34.108 --> 01:29:34.900 align:middle line:90%
which you're right.

01:29:34.900 --> 01:29:37.710 align:middle line:84%
This trapdoor happens also
to be a secret key, too.

01:29:37.710 --> 01:29:41.790 align:middle line:84%
Because that's how we
implemented the commitment.

01:29:41.790 --> 01:29:44.010 align:middle line:84%
And it has a secret
key corresponding

01:29:44.010 --> 01:29:47.030 align:middle line:90%
to this same public key.

01:29:47.030 --> 01:29:47.750 align:middle line:90%
You see?

01:29:47.750 --> 01:29:51.010 align:middle line:84%
So I guess what's
the problematic part

01:29:51.010 --> 01:29:56.590 align:middle line:84%
is that the hash key consists
of a public key of a fully

01:29:56.590 --> 01:29:59.040 align:middle line:90%
homomorphic encryption scheme.

01:29:59.040 --> 01:30:07.152 align:middle line:84%
And to describe g star,
you need the trapdoor,

01:30:07.152 --> 01:30:08.110 align:middle line:90%
which is not a problem.

01:30:08.110 --> 01:30:09.890 align:middle line:84%
You could just encrypt this
trapdoor under public key.

01:30:09.890 --> 01:30:10.900 align:middle line:90%
It's a fresh public key.

01:30:10.900 --> 01:30:13.020 align:middle line:84%
You can encrypt any--
this is kind of fixed

01:30:13.020 --> 01:30:14.530 align:middle line:90%
compared to this public key.

01:30:14.530 --> 01:30:18.090 align:middle line:84%
But you also need to encrypt
the secret key corresponding

01:30:18.090 --> 01:30:20.210 align:middle line:90%
to this public key.

01:30:20.210 --> 01:30:25.080 align:middle line:84%
And for that, we need to
rely on circular security.

01:30:25.080 --> 01:30:26.940 align:middle line:84%
So let me just
quickly write what

01:30:26.940 --> 01:30:30.890 align:middle line:84%
I mean by circular security,
and then we'll break.

01:30:30.890 --> 01:30:32.825 align:middle line:90%
So I'll write it here.

01:30:32.825 --> 01:30:42.110 align:middle line:90%


01:30:42.110 --> 01:30:51.770 align:middle line:84%
It just means that public key
and encryption of the secret key

01:30:51.770 --> 01:30:58.400 align:middle line:84%
is indistinguishable from
public key and encryption

01:30:58.400 --> 01:31:01.610 align:middle line:90%
of, let's say, the all-zeros.

01:31:01.610 --> 01:31:05.580 align:middle line:84%
And let me just mention
that secret key here

01:31:05.580 --> 01:31:07.860 align:middle line:84%
you can think of
it as lambda bits.

01:31:07.860 --> 01:31:10.400 align:middle line:84%
Let's say a security
parameter number of bits.

01:31:10.400 --> 01:31:14.870 align:middle line:84%
Then if we think of
bit-wise encryption,

01:31:14.870 --> 01:31:16.530 align:middle line:84%
it's like encryption
of secret key,

01:31:16.530 --> 01:31:18.988 align:middle line:84%
the first bit of the secret
key encryption, the second bit,

01:31:18.988 --> 01:31:21.500 align:middle line:90%
it's like lambda encryptions.

01:31:21.500 --> 01:31:23.635 align:middle line:84%
And this also, I
mean, encryption of 0,

01:31:23.635 --> 01:31:25.010 align:middle line:84%
encryption of 0,
encryption of 0.

01:31:25.010 --> 01:31:27.160 align:middle line:90%
We do everything bit by bit.

01:31:27.160 --> 01:31:32.140 align:middle line:84%
And the assumption is that you
have an FHE scheme so that you

01:31:32.140 --> 01:31:36.280 align:middle line:84%
cannot distinguish between
whether the lambda encryptions--

01:31:36.280 --> 01:31:40.450 align:middle line:84%
each one encrypts a bit--
whether these encryptions are

01:31:40.450 --> 01:31:42.820 align:middle line:84%
all-zero or whether they
correspond to the bits

01:31:42.820 --> 01:31:44.710 align:middle line:90%
of the secret key.

01:31:44.710 --> 01:31:47.440 align:middle line:90%
So that's an assumption.

01:31:47.440 --> 01:31:52.760 align:middle line:84%
By the way, so the
question is, well,

01:31:52.760 --> 01:31:56.290 align:middle line:84%
do we have FHE that have
circular security, question

01:31:56.290 --> 01:31:56.900 align:middle line:90%
mark?

01:31:56.900 --> 01:31:59.470 align:middle line:90%
Well, we do under an assumption.

01:31:59.470 --> 01:32:01.810 align:middle line:84%
It's called circular
secure learning with error.

01:32:01.810 --> 01:32:05.830 align:middle line:84%
So we just move this circular
secure into the assumption.

01:32:05.830 --> 01:32:07.940 align:middle line:84%
We believe this
assumption to be true.

01:32:07.940 --> 01:32:09.020 align:middle line:90%
We don't know.

01:32:09.020 --> 01:32:12.650 align:middle line:84%
But actually, we
can remove all this.

01:32:12.650 --> 01:32:15.020 align:middle line:84%
It turns out we don't need
to put the secret key there.

01:32:15.020 --> 01:32:19.450 align:middle line:84%
And there's a follow-up
work that did more work,

01:32:19.450 --> 01:32:21.820 align:middle line:90%
a lot of lattice work.

01:32:21.820 --> 01:32:24.010 align:middle line:84%
Here, I didn't
talk at all about--

01:32:24.010 --> 01:32:26.470 align:middle line:84%
I mean, as long as you have
fully homomorphic encryption

01:32:26.470 --> 01:32:28.190 align:middle line:84%
that's circular
secure, we're all set.

01:32:28.190 --> 01:32:30.460 align:middle line:90%
I don't care what this is like.

01:32:30.460 --> 01:32:31.960 align:middle line:84%
But then there's
follow-up work that

01:32:31.960 --> 01:32:34.750 align:middle line:84%
looks at the specific fully
homomorphic encryption

01:32:34.750 --> 01:32:37.660 align:middle line:84%
scheme based on lattices and
does a bunch of lattice tricks

01:32:37.660 --> 01:32:40.610 align:middle line:84%
to remove the need
for circular security.

01:32:40.610 --> 01:32:42.800 align:middle line:84%
So this is something
I didn't show you,

01:32:42.800 --> 01:32:47.140 align:middle line:84%
but there is a follow-up
work that does that.

01:32:47.140 --> 01:32:49.120 align:middle line:90%
So I want to take a break.

01:32:49.120 --> 01:32:51.300 align:middle line:84%
But before a break, are
there any questions?

01:32:51.300 --> 01:32:51.800 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:32:51.800 --> 01:32:53.633 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So here, the
size of the circuit T

01:32:53.633 --> 01:32:55.747 align:middle line:84%
has to be large enough
to contain this--

01:32:55.747 --> 01:32:56.580 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Good.

01:32:56.580 --> 01:32:57.310 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

01:32:57.310 --> 01:32:59.122 align:middle line:84%
So good question,
very good question.

01:32:59.122 --> 01:33:01.330 align:middle line:84%
Guys, you're really having
fantastic questions today.

01:33:01.330 --> 01:33:01.850 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

01:33:01.850 --> 01:33:04.390 align:middle line:90%
So going back, what is this T?

01:33:04.390 --> 01:33:07.620 align:middle line:84%
This T is the number of bits
needed to represent this g star.

01:33:07.620 --> 01:33:13.170 align:middle line:84%
Because in the analysis,
I convert the 0 to the T

01:33:13.170 --> 01:33:16.270 align:middle line:84%
with g star, with the
decryption of g star.

01:33:16.270 --> 01:33:19.030 align:middle line:84%
So this is exactly what
T is in the analysis.

01:33:19.030 --> 01:33:25.850 align:middle line:90%


01:33:25.850 --> 01:33:30.030 align:middle line:90%
Any further questions?

